The dining room was the same as always—formal but slightly outdated, with lace placemats and a chandelier that cast warm light over the table. The smell of roasted turkey, cinnamon, and sage filled the room, mingling with the low hum of conversation.

My mom wouldn’t have it any other way. The turkey sat as the centerpiece, surrounded by an array of dishes that were as much a reflection of her pride as her hospitality.

I sat next to Liam with my dad across from me, my mom directly across from Liam, her posture straight and her smile stretched into something too polished to feel real. She held her wine glass delicately, her fingers curling around the stem in a way that looked effortless but wasn’t. Nothing about my mom was ever effortless.

Liam, however, looked completely at ease. His sleeves were rolled up just enough to show the strong lines of his forearms, and his smile carried a warmth that seemed to wrap the room in a false sense of comfort. He wasn’t just here for dinner; he was performing, seamlessly inserting himself into my family dynamic as if he’d been a part of it for years.

“So, Liam,” my mom began, her tone light and bright, her wine glass already half-emptied, “Caroline tells me you’re on the hockey team. That must be... intense.”

