Liam leaned against the railing, his hands in his pockets, watching me with that easy, half- smirk that always made me feel like he was in on a joke I didn’t know yet. The soft glow of the porch light made his eyes seem darker, more focused, as he finally broke the silence.

“So,” he said, his voice low but laced with dry humor. “Your mom sucks.”

I let out an involuntary laugh, my breath visible in the chilly air. “Liam,” I said, half- scolding, but I couldn’t keep the grin off my face.

“What?” he said, raising his hands in mock innocence. “I’m just saying what we’re both thinking.”
“You’re not supposed to say it out loud,” I said, shaking my head, though I was still laughing. He stepped closer, his smirk softening into something warmer. “Hey, I’m not judging you.
She’s… a lot, but you never let her get to you.” His hand brushed lightly against my arm, a
gesture that felt more grounding than I wanted to admit. “And for the record, I’m impressed. Seriously. I had to use all my training.”

