His words settled over me, warm and heavy. I didn’t know how to respond, so I just nodded, my gaze drifting to the stars scattered across the cold night sky.

“Tell me how to convince you?” Liam asked me seriously. No innuendo, no command to obey, just his voice, low and steady, cutting through the chill of the night.

I glanced at him, caught off guard. For once, there wasn’t a trace of his usual teasing or arrogance. Just a quiet intensity that made it impossible to dismiss him.

“Tell me about your family,” I said instead, deflecting.

He raised an eyebrow, leaning back against the railing. “That’s not an answer.”

“I know,” I admitted, wrapping my arms around myself. “But… I want to know. You don’t talk about them much.”

Liam was quiet for a moment, his gaze fixed on the dark expanse of the backyard. The silence stretched, and I started to wonder if he’d brush the question off entirely. But then he spoke, his voice softer than before.

“My mom’s… distant,” he said, choosing his words carefully. “She’s the type who’s more concerned with appearances than actual relationships. She likes to show me off when I’m doing something impressive—captain of the hockey team, good grades, whatever. But if I screw up, or if I’m not useful to her image…” He trailed off, his lips pressing into a thin line. “She’ll love you, though. Once she sinks her claws in you, I’ll need to fight for time on your schedule.”

I glanced up at him, “I’m sorry”

“It’s fine,” he said quickly, waving a hand as if to dismiss the thought. “I’ve gotten used to it. My dad’s the same, but in a different way. He’s not as focused on appearances—he’s more about results. Success. Discipline. As long as I’m doing what’s expected of me, he’s happy. If I step out of line…”

He didn’t finish the sentence, but the implication was clear. “That sounds difficult,” I said quietly, unsure of what else to say.
“I don’t know,” he said, his gaze shifting to me. “It’s just how they are. I’ve learned how to play the game.”

“That doesn’t make it okay,” I said, echoing his earlier words about my mom.

He smiled faintly, a shadow of amusement flickering across his face. “No, it doesn’t. But it’s not all bad.”

“What do you mean?” I asked, curious despite myself.

“They’ll love you,” he said simply, as if it was the most obvious thing in the world. I blinked, startled. “What?”
“My parents,” he said, his voice steady. “They’ll love you. You’re exactly the kind of person they want me to be with. Polished, smart, put-together. You’re like a PR wet dream. You’ll fit right into their world.”

I stared at him, my mind racing. So there it was. I was just there to complete the picture— exactly the role I once told him I’d never play.

“You’re wrong,” I said, my voice trembling slightly. “I’m not… polished. Or put-together. And I don’t fit into that world.” I waved my hand at nothing.

“Yes, you are,” he said firmly, his eyes locking onto mine. “You don’t see it, but I do. You’re perfect, Caroline. I wish you could realize it.”

The weight of his words pressed down on me, filling the air between us. I didn’t know what to say, how to respond to the intensity in his voice, the certainty in his gaze, not when the only louder voice in my head was telling me “he’s too charming.”

“Tell me how to convince you,” he repeated, softer this time. “Tell me what it would take to get you to come with me.”

I hesitated, my heart pounding in my chest. “I don’t know,” I admitted.

He stepped closer, his presence warm despite the cold. “Then let me figure it out,” he said. “Let me show you that you belong with me. That you’re already enough.”

For a moment, I let myself believe him. I let myself imagine a world where he was right, where I could walk into his family’s holiday party and feel like I belonged. The thought was terrifying—and strangely comforting.

“Okay,” I said finally, my voice barely above a whisper.

His smile was slow, deliberate, and for the first time all night, I felt the knot in my chest start to loosen.

“Good,” he said softly. “You won’t regret it.”

The air between us felt lighter now, the weight of the conversation easing as Liam leaned back against the railing, his smirk returning. I wrapped my arms around myself, staring out at the dark yard, trying to process everything he’d said.

“It’s funny,” I murmured, breaking the silence. “You’d think with parents like ours, we’d be better at figuring this out.”

Liam raised an eyebrow. “Figuring what out?”

“Life. Relationships. All of it,” I said, shrugging. “But instead, it feels like we’re just… fumbling around in the dark, hoping we don’t turn into them.”

He chuckled, the sound low and warm. “Yeah, well, at least we both have terrible parents. That’s something.”

I snorted, shaking my head. “Not sure if that’s a badge of honor.”

“Why not?” he teased, stepping closer. “It’s like a club. Exclusive membership. Terrible Parents Anonymous.”

I rolled my eyes, but I couldn’t help the small smile tugging at my lips. “You’re ridiculous.”

“Maybe,” he said, sliding an arm around my waist and pulling me into his side. “But if we’re going to have terrible parents, we might as well turn it into something useful.”

I tilted my head to look up at him, raising an eyebrow. “Useful how?” He smirked, his tone turning playful. “We’ll do better.”
The words hung in the air, simple but heavy, and for a moment, I let myself lean into him, letting his warmth chase away the chill of the night. But as I looked up at him, his face half- shadowed by the porch light, the knot in my chest tightened again.

We’ll do better. He said it like it was the most natural thing.

I wish there wasn’t any weight to his words, at if there weren’t a promise of a future we couldn’t fulfill.

I looked away, my gaze drifting to the stars scattered across the cold night sky. Liam meant it, I could tell. But promises like that were too big, too fragile. They cracked under pressure, and I wasn’t sure I could handle it when they did.

“Yeah,” I said softly, more to myself than to him. “We’ll see.”

He didn’t push, didn’t ask me what I meant. He just tightened his arm around me, pulling me closer as we stood there in silence.

Friday morning came too quickly.

The house stirred earlier than I wanted, the sounds of my mom bustling around the kitchen waking me from a light, restless sleep. I hadn’t snuck into Liam’s room last night—too risky
—but I could still feel the phantom heat of his arm around me from the patio, his words echoing in my head.

By the time I made it downstairs, Liam was already in the kitchen, sleeves rolled up, helping my dad with breakfast. Pancakes, bacon, scrambled eggs—the works. It was weird seeing them side by side, my dad quietly focused, while Liam made small talk, effortlessly folding himself into the routine.

We spent most of Friday avoiding my mom’s sharp edges. A long walk around the neighborhood—cold air biting at my cheeks, Liam’s hand wrapped tightly around mine. Later, we watched a movie in the living room while my parents argued upstairs about something trivial. Liam pulled me close, his arm draped over my shoulders, his thumb tracing soft circles against my collarbone.

Saturday was more of the same. Quiet, restrained. My mom in her best hosting mode, my dad floating on the edges, trying to make everyone comfortable without really stepping in. We left mid-afternoon, my mom offering a polite goodbye at the door, complete with her usual sharp warning masked as advice.

“Drive safe. And don’t let traffic stress you out, Caroline—you know how you get.”

I felt Liam tense beside me, but he just smiled. “We’ll be fine. Thank you again for having me.”

Her smile faltered for half a second, but she pulled it back, waving as we stepped off the porch. My dad gave me a tight hug, whispering, “Call me when you get back to campus,” before Liam opened the car door for me.

The drive back was quieter than the drive there, but in a comfortable way. I leaned my head against the window, the November landscape rushing past in a blur of browns and grays.
Liam had one hand on the steering wheel, the other resting on my knee, his thumb moving in slow circles.

“Not so bad, right?” he asked eventually, breaking the silence.

I didn’t answer right away, unsure how to sum it up. “It was… Thanksgiving.” He chuckled. “I’ll take that as a win.”
But as we neared campus, the quiet shifted. I expected him to turn toward my dorm, but instead, he veered off, heading toward his place.

I opened my mouth to say something—Shouldn’t I go drop off my stuff first?—but the words stuck in my throat. It wasn’t like I didn’t know where we were going. It wasn’t like I didn’t want to be with him. I did.

But still, the decision had been made before I could even think to ask.

We pulled into the hockey house, the parking lot almost empty. Liam grabbed my bag from the trunk, slinging it over his shoulder like it was nothing.

“C’mon,” he said, his hand brushing against mine. “You’re staying with me tonight.” It wasn’t a question.
I nodded, my chest tightening in a way I couldn’t quite explain. “Figured.”

We made our way inside, the building eerily quiet with most students still away for the holiday weekend. His dorm smelled like him—clean laundry and that subtle cologne I always associated with safety, even when I knew I shouldn’t.

He set my bag down in his room, already moving to open his dresser drawers, pulling out one of his hoodies and a pair of sleep shorts that I kept here.

“Here,” he said, tossing them to me. “Change and get comfortable.”

I obeyed, moving to the bathroom and changing into his clothes. When I came back, he was stretched out on his bed, flipping through Netflix as if this was the most natural thing in the world.

I crawled into bed next to him, letting him pull me against his chest. His hand found its way to my waist, fingers tracing soft circles against my hip.

For a moment, the house, my mom, the entire weekend felt like a distant memory. There was just Liam, warm and solid beneath me.

He pressed a kiss to my temple, his voice low and rough against my ear. “Finally back where I can fuck you whenever I want.”
I felt my stomach twist, the words sinking deep, leaving behind something heavy and warm. I didn’t respond. I didn’t need to.

I let my eyes flutter shut, his arm tightening around me, and let him pull me under. Because it was easier this way.
Letting him make the decisions. Letting him take control.
Because I wasn’t sure I remembered how to make those choices for myself anymore.

Chapter 30.


It hit me all at once.
Not in some grand, cinematic way. There was no thunderous realization, no breathless gasp, no dramatic collapse beneath the weight of it.
It was quieter than that.
Quieter, and far more dangerous.

Liam had disappeared into the locker room, leaving me outside the rink to wait for him. He’d told me not to go anywhere, and I hadn’t even considered disobeying.

So I sat. My textbooks open, pages stiff with neon ink. My fingers traced the crease beside a highlighted sentence, but my mind had long since abandoned the words. My brain couldn’t seem to hold shape anymore—not around definitions or outlines or midterm review prompts. Not when the storm was curling so silently in my chest.

It had started earlier—when Liam pulled me onto his lap after practice, tucking me against his damp jersey, one arm braced around my waist while he half-listened to whatever Ethan was saying. I barely registered their conversation. I just sat there, inhaling salt and ice and whatever cologne clung to his skin, lulled by the rhythm of his breathing, the steady rise and fall beneath my cheek.

It deepened when he draped his jacket over my shoulders without a word as we stepped into the wind outside. I hadn’t even realized I was cold until his warmth settled against me.
And now, here I was—waiting. Still. Anchored by him in ways I couldn’t undo. The truth pressed against my ribs like a hand.
I loved him.

My throat cinched tight.
I had never said it. Not aloud. Not even in the safe dark corners of my own mind. Because to love him felt like stepping over a line I didn’t know I was standing on.

Before this, I could pretend. I could tell myself I was just surviving him. That what we had was circumstance. Pressure. Something that happened to me, not something I chose.

But I had chosen him, over and over again. From the time I stated my own demands over dinner, to the way I reached for his hand when I was spiraling. In the way his voice steadied me. In the way I felt safer pressed against him than anywhere else in the world.

My fingers curled into the fabric of his jacket, gripping tight. I was terrified.
Terrified of how deep this had settled. Terrified of what it meant.
Terrified of losing him.

The thought struck hard—sharp and fast and breath-stealing. I tried to imagine it: a life without him, without the security of his rules, his voice, his certainty. A life where I wasn’t his.

I couldn’t. I didn’t want to. I clenched my jaw, willing my breath to steady.

So much had already been taken from me—my body, my freedom, my edges. But this? This was mine.
Loving him was mine.

The locker room door swung open.

I straightened instinctively, my eyes drawn to the movement. Liam stepped out, gym bag slung over his shoulder, hair still damp and curling slightly at the edges. His gaze found me instantly. His lips curved into a soft, satisfied smile.

He walked toward me like he always did—unhurried, self-assured, as if there was never any question I’d still be waiting. Of course, I was; he knew I would be.

He stopped in front of me, tilting his head. “What?” he murmured. I stared up at him, fingers still fisted in his jacket.
I could say it. Let it fall into the space between us. Let it become real. But now wasn’t the time. Instead, I shook my head. “Nothing.”

He watched me, that unreadable look in his eyes. Then he reached down, cupped my chin in his hand, and tilted my face up.

“You’re thinking too much,” he said quietly.

A shaky laugh escaped my throat. “And that’s a problem?” His smirk deepened. “Not if you’re thinking about me.”

I was. God, I was.

I looked up at him, at the sharp cut of his jaw and the certainty in his expression. He never doubted himself.

Maybe that’s why he didn’t press. Maybe that’s why he didn’t ask.

Because he already knew.

And somehow, the realization didn’t crush me. It calmed me.
It felt like surrender.
But maybe it was also relief.

The dorm was too quiet without him.

I’d sent it without thinking—Win this one for me.
Just something to remind him I existed. Something to remind myself I still did. He hadn’t replied. Not that I really expected him to. He was gone, off somewhere in upstate New York with the team, lacing his skates and adjusting his shoulder pads while my hands shook just trying to fold laundry.

Chloe was out at the gym. Alex was with her chem study group. I’d told them I had a paper to finish, but that was a lie. I didn’t even open my laptop. I just stood there for a long moment in the middle of our room, jacket still clutched in my hand, like I couldn’t remember what I was supposed to do next.

The silence made it worse. Too many thoughts pressing in at once. Too much space between breaths. I went to the fridge. Nothing but coconut milk, three LaCroix, and a sad-looking Red Bull that probably expired last month. I opened Chloe’s desk drawer. Shut it. Checked under the bathroom sink. Beneath Alex’s bed. My own closet. The shoebox I hadn’t touched since fall.

Nothing. No wine. No leftover seltzers from the last dorm party. No hidden bottle of cheap vodka someone forgot to take home over break. No pills I hadn’t already accounted for.

I wanted Adderall. I wanted Klonopin. I wanted something to numb the ache in my chest before it turned into something sharper. I wanted a drink, even a warm one. Something to slow everything down—my thoughts, my heart, the unbearable noise inside my skull.

Just enough to take the edge off. Just enough to feel still again.

I sat down hard on the edge of my bed, pressing my knuckles into my temple. The throb behind my eyes wouldn’t let up. I lay back, limbs heavy, and stared at the ceiling until the blur stopped shifting. I tried to breathe evenly, but it felt forced. Mechanical.

My heart was racing.

I’m a fucking addict.

The words came uninvited, curling in the back of my mind like smoke. I didn’t want to need anything. Not the pills. Not the alcohol. Not the boy who told me when to sleep, what to wear, how to move through the world in a way that pleased him.

I love my abuser.

No. No, that wasn’t right. He hadn't hurt me in weeks.

He’s my boyfriend. He loves me.

Except he also tells me when I can eat. Who I can talk to. He keeps my prescriptions, counts them like candy, hands them out like I’m a child. He tracks my schedule down to the minute. He decides when I’m allowed to rest.

He’s controlling. He’s just using me.

My mother’s voice rose like a wave inside my chest: Don’t mistake control for love.
She said it like it was fact. Like she knew something I didn’t. Like she could see the cracks I’d buried under perfect grades and quiet obedience.

But she didn’t know him.

She doesn’t know him like I do.

My fingers curled into the blanket beneath me, twisting fabric between my fists. She didn’t see the way he looked at me. The way he held me when I couldn’t stop shaking. The way he kissed my temple when he thought I was asleep. The way he’d handed me his jacket without a word, like he knew I’d need it before I even realized I was cold.

That meant something.

I meant something.

I closed my eyes. But the thoughts didn’t stop. They just kept circling—tight, endless, impossible to outrun. I didn’t know what was true anymore. I only knew what I wanted.

I didn’t want to lose him.

Even if it meant losing everything else. The door clicked open.
I flinched.

Light spilled into the room—warm, artificial, too bright for how dark everything felt inside my head. I hadn’t heard their footsteps in the hall. Hadn’t registered the hum of the elevator.

Hadn’t realized I was still clutching the blanket like it might keep me from floating off the bed entirely.

“Caroline?” Chloe’s voice was soft, cautious, like she wasn’t sure if I was awake. She stepped into the room, holding a smoothie in each hand. Alex followed behind her, a third cup tucked into the crook of her elbow.

Chloe’s brows drew together as she took me in. “Wait—are you crying?” I blinked. Reached up to touch my cheek. My fingers came away damp. I hadn’t even realized.
Chloe crossed the room without waiting for an answer and held out the extra smoothie. I sat up, slowly, my limbs leaden, and took it from her with hands that still trembled a little.

“Thanks,” I mumbled, wiping at my eyes with the sleeve of my hoodie.

She didn’t answer. Just sank down onto the edge of her bed, Alex following without a word. They didn’t crowd me. Didn’t press too hard.

But Chloe’s voice came gently, unwavering. “Hey,” she said. “Talk to us.”
I stared down at the smoothie in my hands. It was starting to sweat, condensation dripping down the side and pooling against my palm. I hadn’t taken another sip.

The room was too quiet. Too gentle. Their concern felt like pressure I couldn’t shrug off. “I need your opinion,” I said finally.
Chloe shifted, the mattress creaking beneath her. Alex didn’t move. She just watched me— calm, steady, unblinking.

I looked at her. “I know you don’t like him,” I said, voice low, “but I also know you won’t bullshit me.”

Her brows drew together just slightly. “Do you think Liam is playing me?” There it was. Out loud.
Neither of them spoke right away. Chloe’s lips parted, but she didn’t say anything. Alex’s expression didn’t change. No gasp. No judgment. Just that same level gaze, like she’d been waiting for me to ask.

I exhaled, slow and uneven. “I just need to know if I’m being stupid.” Chloe let out a breath, hesitant. “Did something happen?”

I didn’t answer right away. Just stared at the condensation gathering on the side of my cup, watching it bead and slide like I could somehow disappear into it.

“My mom said something to me over Thanksgiving.” That alone made both of them look up.
“She cornered me, literally pulled me into the pantry like we were about to talk recipes or something.” I exhaled, slow. “Said Liam can’t be trusted. That he’s too controlling. That I’m not myself anymore.” My voice thinned.

“And the worst part?” I looked between them. “She said I wouldn’t notice - and neither would you. Because he’s too charming.”

The words hung there like fog, too heavy to shake off.

I set the smoothie down on the nightstand, leaned forward, and cradled my head in my hands. My elbows pressed into my knees, my fingers threading through my hair like I could anchor myself there—tight, unmoving, contained.

Alex didn’t hesitate.

“God, I hate your mom,” she said flatly. That startled a bitter laugh out of me.
“She’s exhausting,” Chloe added, scooting closer on the bed. “Caroline, listen to me. Your mom is threatened. When people like her start to lose their power, they manipulate. That’s what this is.”

I didn’t lift my head.

“Liam loves you,” she went on, her voice firmer now. “It’s so fucking obvious it’s almost obnoxious. He worships the ground you walk on, and yeah, he’s intense—but you’re not some helpless girl being brainwashed. You’re smart. You’re sharp. You don’t just disappear.”

I shook my head, hands still tangled in my hair.

“But she’s right about one thing. I’m not okay.” My voice cracked. “I haven’t been okay for a long time, and I’ve gotten really good at hiding it.”

Silence.

I finally looked up, my eyes locking with Chloe’s.

“She said you wouldn’t see it. That none of you would. And what if she’s right about that too?”

The words hung there, quieter this time. Not defiant. Not even angry.

Just afraid.

They didn’t say anything right away.

Then Chloe moved first—rising from her bed and crossing the short space between us without a word. Alex followed, less hurried but just as sure. They sank down on either side of me, flanking me on the edge of the mattress, their weight dipping into the blanket like they meant to hold me steady by just being there.

I didn’t look at either of them. Just kept my eyes fixed on the floor, my hands now limp in my lap, still faintly sticky from the smoothie.

“What do you mean,” Chloe asked gently, “about not being okay?”

Her voice didn’t press. It folded around me like something warm. Like it wasn’t trying to pry, just trying to keep me from slipping further.

I swallowed hard.

“I mean—” I stopped. Reset. “I mean I’ve been running on fumes for months. Like I don’t know who I am when I’m not managing something. Hiding something. Keeping it together.”

Alex’s hand brushed my knee, grounding.

“I mean I barely sleep unless I’m with him. I forget to eat unless he reminds me. I take what I’m supposed to take, when he says to, because I don’t trust myself not to mess it up.”

I glanced down at my hands.

“I feel like I’m always a step away from falling apart. And I don’t even know when that started.”

Neither of them moved. But they didn’t let go, either.

Alex was quiet for a moment, her fingers still loosely hooked over her knee. Then her voice came low, steady.

“Are you… using again?”

I shook my head before she could finish the sentence, but the denial felt flimsy. Incomplete. “No. I’m not,” I said. “But I want to. All the time.”
My voice barely made it past my throat.

“I tore through the whole dorm earlier. Chloe’s drawers. Alex, I even checked under your bed. I didn’t even know what I was hoping to find—just… something to take the edge off. Something to make it all stop.”

Chloe didn’t speak. She just leaned in, wrapping her arms around me without warning. Her head rested gently on my shoulder, the pressure warm and solid and familiar.

“Recovery is hard,” she said softly. “And I think Liam’s guiding you through it. I don’t think he’s controlling you—not in that way. I think he’s the structure you’re clinging to because it’s safer than unraveling.”

I closed my eyes. Let the weight of her settle against me. Alex exhaled, long and audible. “I worry about you.”
I turned slightly toward her, and she met my gaze without flinching.

“Do I think this borders on codependency?” she asked, one brow arching. “Oh yeah. Big time.”

I almost smiled.

“But like Chloe said,” she continued, “I think it’s either this… or actual rehab. Rehab wouldn’t have helped. You’d have smiled your way through it, told them exactly what they wanted to hear, and walked out looking cured—but still falling apart. Liam at least gives you decent coffee and your dignity.”

There was no judgment in her voice. Just truth. And something that sounded dangerously close to love.

Chloe shifted beside me, squeezing my arm once before letting go.

Alex nudged me lightly with her shoulder. “Alright. Enough spiraling for one night. We’re doing a girls’ night in.”

I blinked at her. “What?”

Chloe perked up immediately. “Spa night. Face masks, popcorn, something trashy on Netflix.”

“I don’t—”

“No,” they said in unison.

Alex smirked. “We’re not asking.”

Chloe stood up and stretched, already pulling her hair into a bun like it was settled. “You, me, Alex, and enough sugar to make us sick.”

I stared at them, caught between disbelief and the smallest flicker of something warm in my chest.

“Come on,” Alex said, already moving to rummage through Chloe’s drawers. “You survive boys, breakdowns, and academic warfare. You can survive a little cucumber eye patch and

girl talk.”

I huffed a laugh despite myself.

And for the first time all night, it didn’t feel like pretending. The room smelled like lavender and popcorn.
Chloe had unearthed a jade roller from somewhere beneath her makeup stash and was now aggressively smoothing it across her cheek like it owed her money. Alex sat cross-legged on the floor, half-heartedly painting her toenails a shade of pale, icy pink while a trashy reality show played in the background with the volume just low enough to talk over.

My face felt tight from the clay mask drying across my skin. I’d forgotten how strangely comforting it was—this ritual of ordinary girlhood. The shared silence. The stupid commentary. The scent of something sugary and synthetic blooming from Chloe’s exfoliating foot scrub.

There were moments of real laughter. Small ones. Easy ones. Alex making a deadpan comment about the male lead’s tragic haircut. Chloe insisting we rank our worst-ever dates in ascending order of disaster. Someone threw a Sour Patch Kid. It hit the wall and stuck.

I didn’t say much. I didn’t have to. They let me sit curled up in Chloe’s bed, wrapped in a fuzzy blanket and surrounded by the clutter of our friendship—nail polish bottles, half-empty mugs, stray cotton rounds like tiny parachutes abandoned mid-fall.

Chloe tapped my shoulder with the end of her roller. “And what’s the important lesson we learned today?”

Alex didn’t look up from her toes. “We don’t listen to toxic mothers.”

Chloe gave a dry smile. “Even broken people land a hit when they throw enough accusations. Doesn’t mean she was right—just lucky.”

I let out a quiet laugh, small but real.

The ache was still there. It always would be. But for now, it was buried beneath the balm of normal things—pink toes, cheap candy, and two girls who didn’t let me fall apart alone.

And maybe that was enough for tonight.

That night, after Alex had gone to bed in her room and our room fell quiet, Chloe and I lay in our twin beds, the glow from her phone casting faint shadows against the ceiling.

I rolled onto my side, voice barely above a whisper. “So… I think I love him.”

There was a rustle of blankets. Chloe turned to face me. “It’s about time you admitted it. Have you told him yet?”

I snorted. “What do you think?”

She groaned into her pillow. “Ugh. You’re the worst.” “Thanks.”
She propped herself up on one elbow, eyeing me. “What are you thinking? Some grand romantic gesture? Or are you saving it for, like, post-orgasmic bliss?”

I laughed and threw a pillow at her. “I don’t know. I was thinking maybe I should do something nice for him, and just… let it come out naturally.”

Chloe wiggled her eyebrows. “Maybe surprise him by waiting for him when he gets back from his game tomorrow? Wear something he bought you—” her voice dipped into a faux- sultry tone, “—strategically.”

I rolled my eyes. “You think that’s enough?”

She grinned. “I think you know exactly how to make it special.”

I smiled into the dark, the kind that didn’t quite reach my lips but lived somewhere quieter— deeper.
Chloe drifted off first, mumbling something about face masks and lingerie.
I lay there for a long while, staring at the ceiling, my hands tucked beneath the blanket, the ache in my chest no longer sharp but lingering.
Not gone. Just quieter.
And for the first time in a long time, I let myself want something—without apology, without fear.
Just want.
Just him.

Chapter 31.


Why did this dorm always feel so damn quiet?

I sat cross-legged on my bed, my laptop open, the blinking cursor on my half-finished essay like a taunt. I’d been staring at the same sentence for nearly twenty minutes, reading and rereading the words without absorbing them. Each time I tried to focus, my mind drifted somewhere else.

No—to someone else.

Liam.

The absence of him filled the room more than his presence ever had.

He’d left that morning for an away game—hockey bag slung over his shoulder, pressed kiss on my forehead, whispered reminders: “Stick to the plan. Eat, study, don’t fall behind. I’ll check in.”

And he had.

Like clockwork, my phone buzzed next to me, vibrating against the bedspread with a familiar insistence.

I didn’t have to check it to know.

Still, my fingers reached for it, my chest tightening.

Liam: How’s the essay coming?

The weight of the question settled on my shoulders. It wasn’t really a question. Not from him.

I hovered over the keyboard, trying to figure out the right balance between honesty and… whatever else he expected.

Caroline: Slow. I can’t focus.

I hit send before I could overthink it, but the response came too quickly.

Liam: Caroline.

I read it again.

Just my name. That was all it took. I could hear his voice in my head—soft but edged, that calm warning laced beneath the surface, like a leash tightening.

My stomach twisted.

Another buzz.

Liam: Send me what you have. I’ll review it on the bus.

There it was. That push—gentle, but immovable. The way he always knew when to step in, when to take over.

I copied the mess of half-formed thoughts and pasted them into an email, my hands trembling slightly as I hit send. It wasn’t good enough. I knew that. He’d tell me so.

But it was done.

Caroline: Sent.

The dots blinked for a moment before his reply appeared.

Liam: Good girl. I’ll look at it before the game.

My chest loosened—just slightly. That familiar swirl of guilt and relief pooled in my stomach, equal parts comfort and something colder. Even from miles away, he was still here. Still watching. Still guiding.

I placed the phone face down on the bed, but the phantom buzz lingered in my mind.

Truthfully, I wasn’t thinking about my paper at all. I was thinking of what I had planned for when he returned from his away game. Yet, something was still lurking in the back of my mind - the need for my idea to be perfect versus the only person who might actually be able to help. Sighing in defeat of common sense, I reached for my phone again, my thumb hovering over my contacts. I scrolled past Alex’s name, past Chloe’s name, until I landed on Ethan.

I hesitated.

It was a bad idea. A terrible one.

But if anyone knew what Liam liked—what would make this perfect—it was Ethan. My thumb hovered before I gave in.
Caroline: Hey… random question. What’s Liam’s favorite food? The reply came faster than I expected.
Ethan: Wow. Didn’t think I’d see the day. You need my help?

I clenched my jaw.

Caroline: I’m serious.

Ethan: I am too. Big day for us.

I sighed, fingers tightening around the phone.

Caroline: Forget it.

But before I could exit the chat—

Ethan: Fine. Steak. Rare. Roasted potatoes. Red wine. But… why? I exhaled.
Caroline: I wanted to do something nice. Surprise him when he gets back. A beat of silence. Then:
Ethan: That’s it? A steak dinner?

The teasing tone radiated through the screen.

Caroline: It’s not the only thing.

Ethan: Oh? Do tell.

I didn’t respond.

Ethan: C’mon. When have I ever led you astray? A bitter laugh almost slipped out.
Caroline: It’s a secret.

Ethan: I’ve known Liam most of my life. You’ve known him, what? A few months? Who do you think knows him better?

The words stung. And worse? He wasn’t wrong. I stared at the screen, my chest constricting.
Caroline: Ugh. Fine. I’m recreating my first time… A long pause.
Then—

Ethan: Holy shit.

Ethan: He’s going to love that.

A hollow kind of pride filled me. This was good. This was perfect.

Caroline: Obviously.

But the next message made my heart stall.

Ethan: Don’t get cocky. If you really want to make it perfect—push it further. My fingers froze.
Caroline: Further?

His reply came almost instantly.

Ethan: Use your first’s name.

The words hit me like a gut punch. Caroline: Absolutely not. He’d lose it. Ethan: Exactly.
I stared at the message, bile rising in my throat.

Caroline: That’s insane. He’d be furious.

Ethan: Yeah, for a second. But then? He’d get it. He’d know what you were doing. It’d flip that switch in him. Give him the ultimate high.

I squeezed the phone tighter, my eye squinting as if Ethan could feel my glare through cell towers and satellites.

Ethan: You’re giving him the chance to erase whoever that was. To rewrite that memory. To take it back—properly—this time.

I felt sick. The idea slithered under my skin, wrong but… sticky. Like it wouldn’t let go.

Caroline: He’d be pissed.

Ethan: At first. But then he’ll see it. He’ll know it’s the ultimate gift. You’re telling him he’s the only one who matters now. The only one who ever did.

My chest ached.

Caroline: And if you’re wrong?

Ethan: I’m not. He loves you. This will show him how much you’re his. Completely. I stared at the screen for what felt like hours before typing the only thing I could.

Caroline: [eye rolling emoji] Whatever, I’ll think about it. The three dots flickered, then his last message appeared.
Ethan: That’s all I ask. But trust me, princess. It’ll drive him crazy—in the best way.

A gif of Like a Virgin followed, Madonna spinning on a stage, her wedding dress billowing. I locked my phone, my hands shaking.
The idea crawled inside me, burrowing deeper than I wanted.

Would Liam see it as the gift Ethan described? Or would it be the thing that finally broke everything?

What if I really didn’t know Liam as well as I thought I did. It’s not like he was an open book and his psyche was far beyond what could be categorized as “normal.”

And that scared me more than anything.

Chloe looked up from her phone, “why do you look like you’re trying to will your phone to talk to you?”

My head snapped up, “huh?”

“You went from happy, determined to your own worse enemies in less than 5 minutes.” She cocked her head to the side, “Mommy Dearest send another signature ‘I’m not mean, Caroline, just truthful’ bullshit texts?”

I have her a small smile. “No, just texting Ethan”

“Ew! Why!? That guy makes crawling under people’s skin a sport - seriously, don’t let him mess with you!”

She was right, why was I entertaining anything he said? Because he knows Liam better than your. I swallowed that thought down.

“You’re right.”

“Obviously. Now don’t you have a big surprise to prepare for? Get to it!” Chloe blew me a kiss and sent me packing. I flashed her a big smile and promised her to text updates. “ And text me if you have questions - not a certified psychopath, ok?” She reminded me as I walked out the door.

The walk to the hockey house felt longer than usual.

The grocery bags dug into my fingers, the thin plastic stretching under the weight, but I barely noticed. My thoughts were too loud—Ethan’s words looping in my head.

Use his name. Push it further.

I shook the thought away, but it stuck—clingy and persistent, like a splinter I couldn’t reach.

The house was empty. The team was still away, scattered across the state, playing their hearts out while I tiptoed through their space, carrying a bottle of wine (courtesy of Chloe), a raw steak, and the vague hope that I could somehow make tonight… perfect.

I unlocked the door using the code Liam had given me months ago—one of those moments where it was hard to tell if it had been a gift or a leash.

The house was quiet, save for the hum of the fridge and the occasional creak of the old floorboards. I set the bags down on the counter, wiping my damp palms against my jeans.

I could do this.

I pulled out the groceries, setting the steak to marinate while the potatoes roasted in the oven. The sharp scent of rosemary and garlic filled the kitchen, grounding me. I clung to the methodical rhythm—slice, stir, season—as if it could drown out the noise in my head.

Push it further.

No, that was a terrible idea.

I pressed the thought down, hard. Ethan was wrong. Liam wouldn’t see it as a gift. He’d see it as betrayal.

Wouldn’t he? Ugh, I hated how my brain worked sometimes. Ethan was stupid, his whole idea was stupid.

I poured a glass of wine—just one—and took a small sip, letting the warmth spread through my chest. It helped, but only a little because the guilt crept in faster than I had expected. Old words, “I can’t trust you with alcohol” bubbled in my mind. No, he couldn’t and here I was proving him right. I placed the glass down and went back to work.

The house felt strange without Liam. Empty, but not in the comforting way. His presence still lingered—in the faint trace of his cologne, in the perfectly arranged stack of books on the coffee table, in the jacket he’d left slung over the back of a chair. Even in his absence, he was here.

I carried the plates upstairs, my heart racing. His room was exactly how I remembered— perfect, clean, deliberate. The dark sheets pulled tight, the desk organized with military precision. It was the kind of room that didn’t allow for mistakes. Navy comforter, a few banner flags, a couple of polaroids (including a cute one of us). It started to feel like another home to me.

I set the plates on the desk, adjusting the candles I’d brought. Their soft glow flickered against the dark walls, transforming the space into something warmer, softer—almost intimate.

Almost.

I pulled the delicate cotton and lace set from one of the shopping bags, the fabric whisper- light between my fingers. It was white—sweet, innocent. Almost -no, barely - bridal. I laid it carefully across the bed, the contrast between it and Liam’s dark sheets sharp and deliberate.

The last touch—the wineglass. I placed it on his nightstand, filled halfway, the deep red catching the candlelight.

Perfect.

But it didn’t feel perfect. Not yet.

I stood in front of his mirror, staring at my reflection. I quickly changed into the lingerie and a dress I had packed just for the occasion.

The dress I wore was simple—elegant, but soft. Liam had bought it for me recently, soft and warm, just like him. It was the kind of thing I would’ve worn back in high school, before all of… this. Before Liam. Before everything. But it was different now. Tailored. Polished. Like me—but better.

At least, that’s what I told myself.

I reached up, fingers grazing the pearl necklace at my throat. It felt heavier tonight, like it was anchoring me to this moment.

I closed my eyes, breathing in deeply. I could do this.
My phone buzzed on the nightstand. I didn’t want to check it, but my body moved before my mind could protest.

Liam: On the way back. Be home soon.

The words sent a jolt through me—equal parts excitement and dread. I typed back quickly.
Caroline: Can’t wait.

But as I set the phone down, another thought clawed its way to the surface—Ethan’s voice, smug and sure.

Use his name.

I swallowed hard, a burn rising in my throat. I hated that a part of me still considered it. That a part of me wondered if Liam would see it the way Ethan claimed.

Would he?

Or would it ruin everything?

I shook my head, trying to push the thought away, but it stuck—twisting tighter.

I hurried about to finish the set up - an old table now took up much of the room but it looked cozy with the water glasses reflecting the soft light. Rosemary and garlic filled the small room.

The sound of the front door creaking open downstairs snapped me out of it. I heard the murmur and load footsteps of the players coming back from a sour loss.

He was home.

I froze, my heart slamming against my ribs as I listened to the heavy thud of his bag hitting the floor, the soft creak of the stairs under his weight.

Each step echoed in my chest.

I stood there, clutching the pearl at my throat, as his footsteps stopped outside the door. A soft click.
The door opened.

And there he was.

Liam stood in the doorway, his duffel bag slung over his shoulder, his hair still tousled from the game. His eyes swept the room—the candles, the table set, the wine on the nightstand— before landing on me.

For a moment, he didn’t say anything.

Then, slowly, that familiar smile spread across his face. Slow. Deliberate. Dangerous. “Well,” he murmured, his voice low, rough. “Look at you.”
I swallowed hard, my fingers tightening around the pearl.

“I wanted to do something special,” I said, forcing the words past the lump in my throat. “For you. Figured it’s the least I could do...” I let my voice trail off.

He dropped his bag to the floor with a heavy thud, stepping inside and closing the door behind him.

The click echoed in the stillness.

He crossed the room slowly, his gaze never leaving mine, until he stood just inches away. His fingers brushed my jaw, tilting my face upward.
“This,” he whispered, his breath warm against my skin, “is perfect.”

I felt my knees weaken, my body betraying me even as my mind screamed to stay steady.

His thumb traced my lower lip, slow and deliberate. “You really planned all this?” I nodded, my throat tight. “I wanted it to be perfect.”
His smile deepened, dark and satisfied. “It is.”

But beneath the warmth in his voice, there was something sharper, something hungry. And for the first time, I wondered if I had given him too much, or not enough.
Liam closed the space between up, lifting my chin as he lips met mine. This felt right. Safe. Loved.

Liam’s fingers lingered on my jaw, his touch deceptively soft, but there was tension beneath it—like he was holding himself back, barely.

“This,” he murmured, voice low and rough, “this is perfect.”

His eyes swept over the room, the table set and the candles flickering, but when his gaze returned to me, it was heavier—brimming with something darker.

His jaw tightened, the flicker of something raw behind his eyes before he schooled his expression back into calm control. “You have no idea how much I needed this.” They had lost. He didn’t have to say the words, I could just tell.

He stepped back slowly, his fingers trailing from my jaw down to the pearl at my throat. He tugged it lightly, the chain pulling against my skin before he let it fall.

Without another word, he plucked the wine glass from the nightstand before settling at the table. He swirled it once, then sipped, eyes never leaving mine.

He gestured to the chair across from him. “Sit. Dinner’s getting cold.” It wasn’t a suggestion.
I sat down, smoothing my dress as I tried to steady my breathing, but my heart raced too fast
—caught between the warmth of his praise and the weight of what I was about to say.

We ate in near silence (no need to bring up the away game), the occasional clink of silverware the only sound. I watched him as he cut into the steak—calm, controlled—every move deliberate. Like this was all part of some plan I hadn’t been told about.

I thought about when to say the words to him and I still hadn’t decided on the best moment. Maybe during dinner? Maybe in bed? Maybe another time… I could hold out for another moment. I hated that I could still hear Ethan’s words echoing in my head—Push it further. Make it real. It tainted everything I had wanted to focus on tonight.

After taking a sip of wine, I regained some semblance of courage:

“I was thinking…” My voice faltered, but I forced it out. “We could… recreate my first time.”

The air between us shifted instantly—something tense and brittle snapping tight. Liam’s fork froze mid-air. He didn’t blink. Didn’t speak.
A beat passed.

Then, low and rough, he said, “Say that again.”

The heat of his gaze pinned me in place, like moving might shatter something fragile.

I swallowed hard. “I thought we could… recreate my first time. The way it should have been.” My voice trembled, but I held his gaze. “With you.”

The silence stretched, thick and suffocating.

Liam set his fork down slowly, deliberately, as if he needed the extra seconds to contain whatever was unraveling beneath the surface.

When he finally spoke, his voice was quieter—but it vibrated with something dangerous beneath the calm.

“You want to rewrite it?” he murmured, leaning forward, elbows on the table. “You want to give me what should’ve been mine?”

I could feel my heartbeat in my throat, thudding painfully. “Yes,” I whispered.
His jaw flexed, the muscle ticking sharply, but there was something almost… pained in his expression. “You don’t know what you’re offering me,” he said, his voice taut.

I squared my shoulder and raised my chin to make myself look more sincere, “I do.”

For a moment, I saw it—his control slipping. A rawness behind his eyes. But then, like always, he reeled it back in.

“This isn’t just some sweet gesture, Caroline,” he murmured, low and rough. “You don’t get to erase the past with candles and good intentions.”

He reached out for my hand, brushing my knuckles with his thumb.

“But you want to give me that?” I watched the way his eyes shone in the darkness, revealing something I hadn’t seen before. “You want to make it mine?”

I nodded, barely able to breathe. “Yes.”

He exhaled slowly, and when he spoke again, the words were softer—but laced with something sharp. A new smile had crept on to his face, this one triumphant, proud, self

assured.

“Then I’ll take it. All of it.” He stood slowly, crossing the space between us with agonizing slowness. He stopped behind me, his hands resting lightly on my shoulders—too light. His breath ghosted over my neck. His hands slid down, fingers grazing the pearl necklace at my throat. He tugged—harder this time—pulling my head back slightly. He kissed my lips the way that sin seeps into the skin - gently but in a way the infects the whole person: mind, body and spirit.

He released the necklace, letting it fall against my collarbone with a soft thud. His hands wrapped around my wrists next, pulling me to my feet, turning me to face him.

His eyes searched mine—intense, almost feral beneath the calm.

“I shouldn’t have to erase anything,” he murmured, his voice raw now, no longer perfectly controlled.

I opened my mouth, but nothing came out.

His hands moved to my waist, fingers digging into the fabric of my dress. “But if this is how I get it—if this is how you give it to me…” He trailed off, his gaze darkening. “Then I’ll make sure you forget it ever happened any other way.”

The words hit me harder than I expected—cutting deep. Because I knew he meant it.

He tilted my chin up, forcing my eyes to meet his. “When I’m done tonight, there won’t be anyone else. No one else who touched you. No one else who mattered.” His voice dropped even lower, almost a whisper. “It’ll be like it was always me.”

There it was—that subtle darkness creeping through the cracks, hidden beneath the soft words. The manipulation, the control, wrapped in something that sounded almost like love.

But it wasn’t love. Not really. It was something more. Consuming in a way that I wanted to never be free of it.

Still, I nodded, even as my hands shook. “Only ever you.”

His jaw relaxed, satisfaction flickering across his face. “You’re mine.”

And then he smiled—slow, deliberate, dangerous, because in that moment, he knew. He had me.

Liam’s hands moved over me like I was something sacred—his to worship, his to ruin.

The air between us was thick, weighted, humming with something deeper than desire. It was reverence. Hunger. A desperate need to consume, to take, to make me his in every way.

His fingers curled around my wrists, guiding me from the table to the bed. The backs of my legs hit the edge of the bed, but he didn’t push me down. Not yet. He stood close, chest rising and falling in slow, measured breaths, his control fraying at the edges.

His eyes—dark, starved—raked over me, devouring every inch. It was almost too much, the way he looked at me. Like I was something fragile and precious, something to be unwrapped, piece by delicate piece, before he tore me apart.

“This,” he murmured, his voice rough, cracking with restraint. “This is exactly how it should’ve been.”

The words settled heavy between us, filling the space like smoke.

