Chapter 32.



Liam’s POV.

The locker room buzzed with leftover adrenaline—voices too loud, the sharp tang of sweat and blood still heavy in the air—but all I could feel was her.

Caroline.

Last night was incredible. Perfect. Better than anything I could have asked for. I could still taste her on my tongue, a lingering sweetness that refused to fade. I could still feel the way her body had trembled beneath me, like a delicate leaf caught in a gentle breeze, before it surrendered and became soft, pliant, perfect in its vulnerability. The way she’d gazed at me— wide-eyed, trusting—held an innocence, as if there was never any doubt. She was mine.

I flexed my hands, recalling the ghostly sensation of her skin beneath my fingers, smooth and warm, her pulse a delicate flutter beneath the pearl necklace as I’d pulled it snug around her throat. The memory was vivid, almost tangible.

I slammed my locker shut with a force that surprised even me, the metallic clang ringing out sharply in the corridor, slicing through the surrounding chatter like a knife through butter.

And then—

“So, how was it? Are you still on that high?” Ethan.

I didn’t turn. My jaw clenched hard enough that my teeth ached.

He moved in anyway, leaning against the locker beside mine, smirking. Always fucking smirking.

“Tell me she really went all out for you, made it special?” I ignored him.
He didn’t like that.

“Let me guess,” he drawled. “Candles, lace, a breathy ‘take me, I’m yours’ speech. She wore white — tell me she wore white.”

He leaned his shoulder against the locker beside mine, voice dropping lower—mocking, but threaded with something sharper.
“She gave you a monologue first, didn’t she? All wide eyes and sacrificial lamb energy. ‘I want you to have it, Liam, the way it should’ve been.’”
He gave a low, theatrical sigh. “And you kissed her like it was holy?”

I finally turned, slow enough to make a point. “Yeah,” I said, letting it hang. “She did.”
I let the silence stretch before I added—

“You done jerking off to my sex life, or should I send you the playlist and a candle so you play make believe?”

“You’re adorable when you think you’re untouchable” His grin widened, feeding off it. “Just wondering, did she follow through?”

I stiffened.

“Followed through on what?” The words came out clipped, sharper than I wanted.

He tilted his head, feigning innocence. “I gave her a little idea. Told her if she really wanted to make it count, she should say his name.”

Silence. My vision tunneled. “During,” he added, too casually.
A red haze bloomed behind my eyes.

My fist slammed into the locker beside his head with a thunderous clang, the metal denting under the force. Conversations around us cut off instantly, the room falling into an unnatural, tense silence.

I grabbed the front of his jersey, dragging him so close our noses nearly touched. "You told her what?" I hissed, my voice quaking with unbridled fury.

Ethan’s smirk didn’t falter. “Relax. She didn’t do it.”

My grip tightened, knuckles straining. “You’re playing with fire.”

“She didn’t even try,” he said, his voice softer now, but still laced with that smugness. “Couldn’t say it. Couldn’t even think it.”

I shoved him hard into the lockers, the metal groaning under the impact. He grunted but didn’t stop.

“That’s the point,” he pressed on, his breath ragged. “She’s so far gone for you, she couldn’t do it.”

The rage twisted deeper, black and suffocating. The idea that he’d even tried to get in her head—tried to corrupt what I had desperately claimed from her— sent a violent fury coursing through me, making my vision blur with an uncontrollable, seething wrath.

“You think this is a fucking game?” I growled.

Ethan’s grin thinned, but it didn’t disappear. “You tell me. You’re the one who’s got her wrapped so tight she didn’t even blink.”

I wanted to hit him. Break something. Break him. But then—
“She didn’t listen to me, Barnette,” he added, softer now. “She only listens to you. I told her to do something, really fucking sold it too, if I might add, and she didn’t even think. Wanna know why?” He didn’t wait for me to answer, “because you weren’t the one telling her what to do.”

That landed. Heavy. Deep.

My chest heaved, my grip loosening just slightly.

Ethan coughed, rubbing his shoulder where I’d slammed him. “She’s yours. It’s fucking obvious now. Isn’t that what you wanted?”

The words sank in, hot and cold all at once. The rage pulled back, leaving something raw, something hollow.

But Ethan wasn’t finished.

He straightened, brushing dust off his jersey. “You should be thanking me.” I glared at him. “For what? Fucking with her head?”
He shrugged. “I was just testing her. Seeing how far she’d go. And she didn’t crack.” He paused, letting that hang between us. “She came running back to you.”

I clenched my jaw so hard it ached, the tension vibrating through my whole body.

“That’s what’s got you, though, right?” he said, softer now, the smugness peeling back just enough to show something sharper beneath. “The fact that I even got in her head. Even for a second.”

The words hit hard, sharper than any punch.

Ethan let out a low breath, wiping the blood from the corner of his lip. “I’m not the problem here, man.”

I grabbed my duffel, every muscle in my body still vibrating with rage, but I didn’t swing. I didn’t say anything else.

He tilted his head, voice low and venomous. “I’m just the mirror —because if I could rattle her with a few pointed text messages, someone else might too.”

My vision narrowed to a single point.

I didn’t respond. Just stared. Silent. Focused. My hands itched at my sides. He knew. The bastard knew exactly where to press.
“You think she’s untouchable,” he went on, softer now. “Because you’ve trained her to be. But you didn’t erase the girl who wanted out—you just buried her under your voice.”

I took a step closer. Controlled.

He didn’t flinch. Not even when I was chest to chest with him. “Keep going,” I said, too calm.
He smirked, teeth stained with blood. “Happily. It’s the only truth you’ll hear from someone who doesn’t want to fuck you or vote for you.”

Another step—then my hand curled around the front of his jersey, hauling him off the locker and slamming him back again with a thunderous crack. The entire room snapped silent.

Ethan grunted, blood at the corner of his mouth, but the grin didn’t die. It sharpened.

“You branded her, sure—but marks fade. All it takes is one night. The right smile. The right hands. She spreads for someone who doesn’t even try, and suddenly all that work of yours?” He clicked his tongue. “Just a fucked-up memory.”

He leaned in, his voice dropping.
“And if I wanted to, Liam—really wanted to—I’d have her begging by the end of the night. You know it.”

A long pause. His eyes gleamed. “I’ll be happy to share the playlist when it happens.”

That was it.

I threw the first punch.

His head snapped to the side, blood streaking the row of lockers behind him. He didn’t go down—of course he didn’t—but it stunned him long enough for me to land the second hit. Then a third.

Ethan shoved me back, wild grin breaking through the blood now smeared across his face. “There he is.”

He swung. I ducked, caught him in the ribs with my elbow, then drove my fist into his jaw again. We collided hard, slamming into the lockers, fists flying—calculated rage on my end, brutal force on his. There was nothing clean about it. Nothing restrained.

This wasn’t a fight. It was a reckoning.

Someone shouted behind us. Didn’t matter. Could’ve been Mason. Could’ve been God. All I could see was red.
I got him on the floor, my knee in his ribs, my hand fisted in the collar of his jersey, dragging him half upright. His lip was split, bleeding down his chin. His eyes gleamed through it.

And still—still—he laughed.

“Fuck, you should be thanking me,” he rasped, blood in his teeth. “She didn’t listen to me. She listened to you. I told her to say another name—she couldn’t even think it.”

My fist hovered midair, trembled. I didn’t hit him again. Not yet.

“Because it wasn’t your voice in her ear,” he murmured, lips split, eyes glinting. “That’s what eats at you.”

I shoved him back and stood, chest heaving, everything in my periphery edged in red.

Ethan coughed, spat blood at the tile, then looked up—grinning through it. “That’s the part that wrecks you, right? That I got close enough to prove it—she’s yours, so completely she doesn’t even bother thinking unless you say so.”

I stood over him, fists shaking at my sides.

“You think I’m the threat?” he went on, voice raw, cruelly soft. “I’m just the test, Liam. The control group.”
Another beat.
Then—
“You’re the fucking experiment.”

I stared at him. Long. Hard. The words rooted in the place I hated most—the one where he wasn’t wrong.

But that didn’t mean he’d walk away clean.

I leaned down, close enough that only he could hear me. “Get near her again,” I said, voice like ice wrapped around a blade, “and you won’t walk out of this locker room.”

He smiled, bruised and bloodied. “Noted.”

Ethan coughed, laughing through blood-streaked teeth. It wasn’t humor—it was provocation, pure and venomous. His lip was split clean open, one eye already swelling. I hadn’t even realized I’d bloodied my own knuckles until I let go of his jersey and felt the sting.

That smug little exhale—like he’d tasted my rage and liked the flavor—was still hanging in the air when Coach’s voice cracked through the entryway.

“Are you two out of your goddamn minds?”

The door slammed against the wall as Coach stormed in, whistle swinging violently against his chest.

“Practice hasn’t even started and I walk into this?”
His boots echoed on the tile. “Blood on my floor, lockers dented, my captain and alternate looking like they just left a fucking bar fight?”

I straightened, breathing hard, chest still heaving with leftover fury. Ethan hadn’t moved. Still slouched, head tilted, blood trailing down his jaw like war paint.

Coach rounded on me first.

“You want to explain this, Barnette?” My jaw tightened.
Mason appeared like smoke—quiet, fast, already between us. One hand on my chest, not pushing, just enough to block.
“Coach, it got out of hand. I was right there. I should’ve—”

“Save it, Davenport,” Coach snapped. “I don’t need my other alternate playing fucking diplomat. You’re here to stop shit like this before it happens.”

Ethan spat blood onto the floor and grinned through it. “C’mon, Coach. Just a little healthy disagreement.”

Coach turned on him so fast Ethan actually flinched.

“You’re on thin fucking ice, Cooper. Real thin. You think because you can take a hit, you’re untouchable?”

He pointed to the locker behind Ethan—the one I’d dented.
“You think that looks good to the Board? You want donors walking through on Friday and seeing this?”

Ethan didn’t answer. He just leaned his head back and grinned wider, like the chaos was worth it.

Coach’s voice dropped—lower, darker.
“I don’t give a shit who threw the first punch. You both breathe wrong today, I’ll have you benched for the next two scrimmages. No captain’s meeting. No rotation. Nothing.”

Mason cut in again, carefully.
“Coach. Let me talk to them. Just give me two minutes.”

Coach looked at him, eyes narrowing.
“Two minutes. And if I hear so much as a locker slam, we run drills until your lungs give out.”

He stalked out without another word.

Mason exhaled once the door slammed. He turned first to Ethan. “You’re an idiot.”

Then to me: “And you’re not much better.”

I said nothing. Just stared at the blood on my hand and wished I’d hit him harder. Mason waited until the door closed again.
Then he turned to both of us, expression flat, voice low. “You two are fucking unbelievable.”
He didn’t yell. He didn’t need to.

He stepped forward, and for a second, I remembered just how big he was—six-five, broad as a wall, and right now, absolutely done with our shit.

“You think Coach is mad now?” he asked, pointing at the dented locker behind Ethan. “Wait till the Dean gets wind. Or the Senator. Or Diane.”
He shot a look at me. “You want to explain this at family dinner?”

Ethan wiped at his lip again and smirked.
“I’ll just tell them Liam found out I made a move on the future Mrs. Barnette — though she didn’t seem too future-tense to me.”

My fist twitched.

Mason threw out an arm, blocking me without flinching.
“Nope. We’re done.” His voice sharpened. “You want to fight? Join the fucking UFC. This is

Holbrook. You two are supposed to be leaders.”

Ethan leaned back, lazy and smug, blood drying on his jaw. “Technically, I never swung.”

Mason rounded on him. “Yeah? But you never shut your mouth either. Which, let’s be real, might be the bigger problem.”

Ethan gave a mock salute. “Message received.”

Mason’s jaw worked. Then, quieter: “Grow the fuck up, both of you.”

He turned to leave, then paused at the door. “Coach wants you, Captain. Now. His office.” Then he was gone.
Coach slammed the door hard enough that the plaque rattled on the wall. “Sit the fuck down.”
I didn’t argue. The chair groaned as I dropped into it, blood still drying on my knuckles.

He paced behind the desk, jaw tight, eyes burning with disbelief. “What the fuck is wrong with you?”

I didn’t answer.

He slammed both hands down on the desk, leaning in. “You’re my captain, Liam. You don’t get to act like some frat house bruiser when your ego gets bruised. You don’t get to throw punches in my locker room like a fucking amateur.”

He wasn’t shouting. He didn’t need to. His voice was flat. Controlled. Lethal.

“You think this shit doesn’t follow you?” He jabbed a finger toward me. “You think this place is just about trophies and fucking bragging rights? Holbrook doesn’t raise goons. It raises men who are supposed to know better.”

His eyes narrowed, voice clipped. “You're the face of this team. And when you act like that in my locker room, it reflects on all of us. The faculty. The program. Every kid who laces up thinking this sport teaches something.”

He let the words hang, then added, quieter, darker:

“Congratulations, Barnette. You just proved the ice doesn’t always cool the blood.” My jaw clenched.
“You want to tank your future over a girl?” he snapped. “Over Ethan?” I flinched, and that was enough for him.

His voice dropped. Low. Cutting.

“Jesus Christ, Liam. I’ve seen you skate through concussions with more composure than that.”

He let the words hang, then stepped back, scrubbing a hand over his face. A long beat passed.
Then, quieter—gruffer—he pulled out the chair across from me and sat down, elbows on knees.

“I’m not asking what he said,” Coach muttered. “I don’t need to know. I know him.” He paused, glancing up. “But I know you, too.”
I didn’t move.

“You’ve always played better angry,” he said. “But this—this wasn’t strategy. This was something else.”

My Adam’s apple bobbed but I said nothing.

He leaned forward, his voice leveling out. “You want to protect her? Fine. Do it. But don’t let your anger write checks your reputation can’t cash.”

Another pause.

“You’ve worked your ass off to get here. Don’t throw it away now. Not for him.”

He stood, nodding toward the door. “You’re benched for the scrimmage. You’ll suit up Saturday. And you’ll lead like you’ve still got a future worth protecting.”

I stood, my shoulders tight.

As I turned to leave, he added, “Liam.” I looked back.
“I don’t know what she is to you. But whatever it is—make sure it’s worth this.”

After practice, after Mason finally stopped trying to patch things up with jokes and ice packs, I sat alone in the locker room, the buzz of the fluorescent lights pressing against my skull like a migraine.

The locker room still reeked of sweat and blood—mine, his, the years between us. Ethan's blood was still on my knuckles. Some of mine was probably still on his shirt.

And yet—I wasn’t sorry. I’d meant every fucking hit.

What I hated most was that he’d smiled through it. Like he wanted it. Like he’d planned it. Like he needed the confirmation that, yes, he still knew exactly how to get under my skin.

He always had.

We’d been fifteen the first time we fought. Doverfield’s winter formal. He’d flirted with the girl I’d been dating at the time—blatantly, in front of everyone. I dragged him outside before I even knew what I was doing. Two black eyes and a dislocated shoulder later, we were still roommates. The dean called it “boys being boys.” I knew better. It wasn’t about the girl. It was about territory.

Ethan never really wanted her. He just wanted to see if I’d bleed for the things I claimed. And now? All these years later? Same fucking pattern.
Ethan had always known where to press. He didn’t even need to use force—just a word, a look, a single offhand comment laced with that particular venom only he could get away with. That was the problem. He didn’t break skin. He slipped beneath it.

And last night?

He’d done exactly what he always did. Pushed. Prodded. Smiled like the devil and whispered in Caroline’s ear just to see what I’d do.

He didn’t care about the girl. Not really. He cared about what she meant to me. What buttons she opened. What kind of man I became when the thing I’d bled to shape was even slightly threatened.

And I gave him what he wanted.

The high I’d been riding—her breath in my mouth, her body trembling under mine, the weight of her final surrender—gone. Poisoned by a single sentence. A single suggestion that Ethan had even glanced in her direction with the intent to corrupt.

He knew exactly what it would cost me. That was the point. That’s why he said it.

But he wasn’t the one who touched her. He wasn’t the one she let inside. He could plant seeds, sure. That was always his game. But she didn’t bloom for him.

She bloomed for me.

And the truth—the one he wouldn't dare say out loud—was that he admired it. The lengths I went to. The precision. The fucking devotion carved out in silence and control. He never had the patience for that kind of power. He’d rather burn the house down and laugh in the smoke than build something worth keeping.

We’d been like this since Doverfield. Two wolves in pressed uniforms and polished shoes. He was the chaos. I was the weapon.

So no—he didn’t rattle me because I thought he could take her. He rattled me because he reminded me how close I still lived to the edge. How thin the line was between control and ruin.

But he wouldn’t push Caroline. Not really.

He’d brush the perimeter. Sneer at the gate. But he knew the rules. Knew what it would cost. And if he forgot? Well, If he valued what was left of his jaw, he wouldn’t.
I leaned back against the dented metal, jaw still tight but heartbeat finally slowing.

Then—
Caroline: Done with practice yet? I’m still at the library
Caroline: You’ll be happy to hear that I think I actually nailed that paper you helped me with.

A pause.

Caroline: Shit. Just realized I haven’t eaten.

Caroline: Have dinner with me?

I read her texts twice, the tension in my jaw easing—not all the way, but enough. She didn’t have to say much. She never did. Not with me. That was the difference. Ethan could posture, could dig, could spin a dozen sharp little knives and call it insight—but she never strayed.
She came back to me without even realizing she’d left. My reply was simple.
Liam: Leaving now.

Liam: Proud of you. I can take another look when I get there.

Liam: Of course. Want me to bring something to the library or should we head over to the eatery for a late dinner? I’m starving.

I slid my phone back into my pocket, jaw tilting with the smallest, quietest smile.

She didn’t know it yet, but she’d already answered Ethan’s question. With every word. Every pause. She was mine. Still soft. Still reachable. Still exactly where I wanted her.

Right where she belonged.

Chapter 33.


Chloe was practically bouncing on her bed, her legs crossed beneath her as she scrolled through her phone, the soft hum of music playing in the background. I stood in front of the mirror, twisting my hair into a loose braid, trying to ignore the nerves fluttering in my stomach.

It had been a week since Liam got back from the away game. A week since… everything.
Since I’d given him everything.

I caught my own reflection in the mirror—eyes wide, a faint flush still high on my cheeks— and swallowed hard. He hadn’t said anything outright, but I could feel it—the shift between us. Something deeper, heavier. Like we’d crossed an invisible line I couldn’t go back from.

“Alright,” Chloe’s voice cut through my thoughts. “I know you’re nervous about tonight, but I have something that’s going to make this whole thing so much better.”

I frowned, turning to face her. “Chloe…”

