I raised an eyebrow. “What is that?”

She unzipped the bag with a dramatic flourish, revealing a custom hockey jersey—navy blue, Liam’s number 15 bold on the back. But instead of his last name across the shoulders, it read:

Barnette’s Girl.

I blinked, my stomach doing a weird flip.

“Chloe.” My voice caught somewhere between a laugh and a gasp. She held it out to me, eyes gleaming. “Tell me this isn’t perfect.”

I hesitated, fingers hovering over the fabric. The letters—bold, white, loud—felt heavier than they should have. “Isn’t this… a little much?”

She scoffed. “Are you kidding? Liam’s going to lose it when he sees you in this. He’ll probably score five goals just to show off.” She waggled her eyebrows. “And who knows, maybe he’ll propose right there on the ice.”

I let out a shaky laugh, but my chest felt tight.

“Come on, Caroline,” Chloe nudged me, her voice softening. “You know he’s obsessed with you. This? This will make his whole night.”

I bit my lip, still hesitating.

I could picture it already—Liam’s reaction. His eyes darkening the second he saw me. That tight grip he got on his stick when he was worked up. The kind of look that made it feel like the air between us was charged, heavy, like something was going to break.

“He’s going to love it,” I whispered. Chloe beamed. “I knew it!”
I took the jersey from her, the fabric soft and cool in my hands. I pulled it over my head, the oversized material swallowing me whole. It hung past my hips, baggy in the sleeves, but that was the point. It wasn’t supposed to fit.

It was supposed to be his.

I caught my reflection in the mirror again.

The bold letters stretched across my back, claiming me in a way that felt louder than anything I’d ever worn. I tugged at the hem, nerves swirling low in my stomach. I should feel embarrassed. Exposed.

But instead, there was a flicker of warmth. Pride.
“You look so good,” Chloe said, grabbing her phone to snap a picture before I could stop her. “Like, seriously. He’s going to die when he sees you.”

I smoothed my braid over my shoulder, the nerves curling tighter now, mixing with something heavier. I wanted him to see me. I wanted him to know I was here for him.

I wanted him to know I was his.

The arena buzzed with energy—sharp, electric, the sound of skates carving into the ice echoing beneath the cheers.

I tugged at the sleeves of the jersey again, my heart racing.

“Caroline,” Chloe nudged me, leaning over the railing. “You’re seriously going to kill him with that thing.”

I gave her a small smile, but my hands were clammy. The letters on my back—Barnette’s Girl—felt heavier now, like they were pressing into my skin, sinking deeper.

I shifted on my feet, scanning the rink as the announcer’s voice boomed through the arena, calling out the starting lineup. The players skated onto the ice one by one, the crowd roaring louder with each name.

And then—he appeared. Liam.
Helmet low over his brow, his dark jersey stretched tight across his shoulders, every movement fluid, controlled, lethal. He skated out onto the ice, gliding with that smooth confidence, his stick tapping against the boards as he circled center ice.

I stood on my tiptoes, heart hammering in my chest, waiting for him to look up. It felt like forever. Like I was holding my breath in the middle of a hurricane.
And then—he turned.

His head tilted up, almost like instinct, and his eyes swept over the crowd—until they landed on me.

I froze.

Time stilled, the noise of the arena dulling into a low hum. He didn’t blink. Didn’t move.
His gaze locked onto me, dragging slowly down—over my face, my shoulders—and then I moved.

I shifted my body, pulling my braid to the side, and turned around, revealing the words I knew he already believed.

Barnette’s Girl.

The bold letters stretched across my back, stitched into the fabric like a brand.

I stood there, shoulders back, heart pounding, but there was something electric curling low in my stomach—pride. I wanted him to see it. To feel it. To know that I wasn’t just wearing this for him—I was wearing it because I was his.

The air felt thick, heavy, like the entire arena had gone silent.

I waited, feeling the weight of his gaze, knowing what was coming.

Then, slowly, I turned back around.

His eyes were still locked on me—dark, sharp, consuming. And I smirked.
It bloomed on my face, slow and deliberate, fueled by a flicker of boldness that hadn’t been there before. A confidence I hadn’t realized I was capable of.

Liam’s entire body shifted.

His grip on his stick tightened, his shoulders squared, and something primal flashed in his eyes—raw, possessive, dangerous.

He smirked back.

The kind of look that promised consequences. The kind that said, I see you. I see exactly what you’re doing.

I tugged at the hem of the jersey, heat blooming low in my stomach, but this time I didn’t feel nervous.

I felt powerful.

“Shit,” Chloe muttered beside me. “You’re seriously going to ruin him.”

