His focus was still pinned on me—on the smirk I wore, on the jersey hugging my frame, on the words stretched across my back that he’d claimed long before I ever put it on.

Even as the game roared to life around him, he didn’t look away.

The corner of his mouth tugged up, slow and deliberate. His eyes darkened, like a switch flipped inside him, and I could feel it from all the way up here—the way that look wrapped around me, watching like I was the only thing that existed. I loved it.

“Caroline,” Chloe giggled. “He’s not going to survive this game.”

I laughed, my heart racing, but my throat tightened. That look—that pure, intense focus— was all for me.

The ref’s whistle blew, signaling the start of the game, but Liam’s eyes stayed locked on mine for a beat longer, like he was warning me: Watch me. This is for you .

And then he was gone—charging forward, aggressive, fast, unstoppable.

The crowd roared as the game began, but all I could see was him. The way he skated, so fluid and powerful, dodging the opposing team, his stick expertly guiding the puck. Every time he

made a play, scored a goal, or checked another player into the boards, he turned—searching for me. Making sure I saw.

Chloe bumped her shoulder into mine, laughing. “He’s showing off. Full-on peacock mode.” “I know,” I murmured, my cheeks flushed.
