By the time we had arrived, the Hockey House was already alive with music, the bass vibrating through the walls, shaking windowpanes, sinking into the floorboards that had long since turned sticky with beer. The scent of alcohol, sweat, and expensive cologne mixed with something sweeter—perfume, cinnamon, the remnants of crushed candy canes from the house’s half-hearted attempt at holiday decor. The December cold had been left at the door, replaced by the artificial heat of too many bodies pressed together, each movement sending another ripple through the current of the party.

Liam’s hand rested at the small of my back, a touch so light it could have been mistaken for an afterthought. It wasn’t. His fingers traced slow, idle patterns against the fabric of my dress,

a silent, absent-minded gesture that was anything but careless. It wasn’t a demand, wasn’t overt possession, but people noticed anyway.

They always did.

The crowd parted as we moved through the house, not in an obvious, deferential way, but instinctively, subconsciously—the way people naturally made room for someone they knew they should acknowledge. It wasn’t forced, wasn’t even intentional on Liam’s part. It just happened.

Because Liam didn’t need to ask for space. He took it.
And people let him.

That was what it meant to be Liam Barnette—not just the hockey captain, not just the golden boy of Holbrook University, but someone who shifted the energy of a room without trying.
The kind of person people adjusted themselves around, often without realizing they had. And I was on his arm.
I caught the flicker of recognition in passing glances, the small shifts in body language as eyes scanned from him to me, then back again. Some were subtle, a quick double-take, a lingering gaze as if trying to place me in a hierarchy that had already been established. Others weren’t nearly as discreet.

I didn’t shrink under their attention.

Not anymore. I met their gazes, letting them look, letting them wonder, because there was no doubt in my mind—I belonged here.

Because Liam had put me here. Because Liam had decided it. And Liam’s decisions were final.

“Finally,” Ethan drawled from across the room, lifting his drink in a slow, lazy salute. His smirk was already in place, sharp and knowing, the kind that always made it impossible to tell whether he was amused or plotting something. His gaze flicked to me first, dragging deliberately over my dress before landing on Liam. “Took you long enough. Figured you two were busy… celebrating.”

Ethan’s words had stopped biting a few week ago. His remark was now met with an acknowledged look of boredom.

Liam’s grip at my waist subtly firmed, his presence grounding, a silent reminder that Ethan’s games were just that—games.

"Barnette!" A chorus of greetings rose around us as we moved into the house. Red cups lifted in salute, hands extended for the complicated handshakes that seemed to exist only between athletes. Liam acknowledged each greeting with practiced charm—a nod here, a brief clasp

of shoulders there, his smile generous but somehow still exclusive, as if each person he recognized should feel particularly honored by his attention.

And they did. I could see it in their eyes, the way they straightened when he approached, how conversations paused and reformed around whatever Liam chose to say. For a twenty-one- year-old college student, he commanded the room with the assurance of someone twice his age.

"Caroline, looking gorgeous as always," said a hockey player whose name I could never remember, his eyes doing a quick sweep of my body that was just shy of disrespectful. He knew better than to cross that line with Liam standing next to me.

"Thanks," I replied, my voice carrying just the right note of friendly dismissal that I'd perfected in recent months. Not rude, but not inviting further conversation either.

Liam's arm slid around my waist, a casual gesture that was also a subtle claim. "New record on the ice this year," he told the player, smoothly redirecting the conversation. "Coach thinks we have a solid shot at championships next year."

I used the moment to survey the room, catching the glances directed our way. The hockey team looked at Liam with something between respect and devotion. The other guys with a mix of envy and admiration. And the girls—the girls looked at him with unconcealed want, and at me with a complicated cocktail of envy, resentment, and calculation, as if trying to determine what exactly had earned me my place at his side.

If they only knew, I sometimes thought, but that wasn't fair. What Liam and I had was more complicated than they could imagine, darker in some ways but also strangely deeper.

"There's Williams," Liam murmured close to my ear, nodding toward a well-dressed junior standing near the improvised bar. "His father's on the board of the Rappaport Fellowship. Let's say hello."

I nodded, allowing myself to be guided through the crowd, which parted before us like we were minor royalty. The path to Williams was direct but unhurried, Liam pausing briefly to accept congratulations on the team's recent victory, to clap a teammate on the shoulder, to exchange knowing looks with seniors who'd once been his rivals but now deferred to his obvious ascendance.

Last fall, during my first weeks at Holbrook, I would have found this performative navigation exhausting. I remembered clearly how it felt then—being pulled into Liam's orbit, overwhelmed by the social choreography, simultaneously anxious and terrified by the attention. After one party early in our relationship, I'd hidden in a bathroom for twenty minutes, my heart racing with anxiety, wondering what someone like Liam Barnette saw in a scholarship freshman like me and if this was just some sort of sick, torturous game to him.

Now I moved through the ritual with practiced ease. I smiled at the right people, laughed at the inside jokes I'd come to understand, accepted compliments with the right balance of gratitude and casual confidence. The little, strapless black dress Liam had bought me hugged my curves perfectly, its expensive fabric a subtle declaration of status. The pearl necklace

rested steady and secure at my throat, a constant reminder that even beautiful things can start as a piece of dirt.

"Caroline," a girl from my calc class greeted me, her eyes flickering to Liam before returning to me. "That dress is amazing."

"Thank you," I said, touching the fabric lightly. "How did your final problem set turn out?"

"Enough to pass," she beamed, clearly pleased I'd remembered our previous conversation. "Got an A-minus."

"She deserved an A," I told Liam, including him in the exchange. "She is meticulous when it comes to numbers."

Liam smiled at the girl, who blushed under his attention. "Caroline has an eye for talent," he said smoothly. "You should consider applying for the class treasurer. I'm on the nomination committee—I'd be happy to put in a good word."

The girl's eyes widened slightly. "That would be incredible. Thank you."

I watched her absorb what had just happened—how a casual party conversation had potentially opened a door to wider recognition. This was Liam's power, the easy way he distributed favor, creating a network of people who owed him something, however small.

We continued our progress toward Williams, and I caught sight of my reflection in a decorative mirror hanging on the wall. The girl looking back at me was polished in a way I never would have achieved on my own—chestnut hair falling in soft waves, makeup subtle but flawless, the black dress transforming my petite frame into something elegant and untouchable. Beside me, Liam looked like he'd stepped out of a magazine, his dark hair artfully tousled, his casual confidence radiating outward like heat.

We looked perfect together. The thought brought a complicated tangle of emotions—pride, discomfort, a strange sense of accomplishment. I'd become what was expected of me, and sometimes I wasn't sure if that was victory or surrender.

"Jack," Liam greeted Williams warmly, extending his hand. "Good to see you. You’ve met Caroline?"

Williams turned, his assessment of me quick but thorough. "Not formally. Nice to finally meet you," he said, taking my offered hand. His grip was firm, his smile practiced. “Heard Liam's been keeping you to himself. What brings you to Holbrook?"

"Business and International Relations," Liam supplied before I could speak, his tone casual but clear—he was establishing my academic credentials, making sure Williams understood I wasn't just arm candy.

"Impressive combination," Williams nodded. "Planning on graduate work?"

"Perhaps," I replied, finding my voice. "I'm interested in the work of certain NGOs that cater to children in war zones or UNICEF, so grad school would need to align with practical goals,

not just lofty academics."

Williams looked genuinely intrigued, and for a moment, the conversation shifted into something real, something about my actual interests rather than my status as Liam's girlfriend. I felt myself relax fractionally, engaging with Williams about a recent lecture on the subject.

Liam watched with approval, his hand never leaving the small of my back. This was part of his vision for us—me as the intelligent, accomplished partner who enhanced his own image. He'd told me once, in a rare moment of unguarded honesty, that he needed someone who could hold her own in any room. "Beauty isn’t enough — I could find that anywhere," he'd said, tracing my jawline with his finger. "I want substance, Caroline. Someone who matches me. Someone perfect in every way."

It was flattering, in its way. Controlling, but flattering.

The conversation with Williams turned to the Rappaport Fellowship, and I recognized my cue to step back slightly, letting Liam take center stage. This was the dance we did—me showcasing my intelligence just enough to reflect well on Liam, then yielding the floor when the conversation turned to his ambitions. I'd learned the steps over months of parties and dinners, understanding intuitively when to shine and when to support.

As Liam and Williams discussed the fellowship's selection criteria, I scanned the room again, locating the familiar blonde bob of Chloe's hair near the dance floor. She caught my eye and waved enthusiastically, gesturing for me to join her. I gave her a small nod, then returned my attention to the men's conversation, waiting for an appropriate moment to extract myself.

"Jack knows Senator Whitman personally," Liam was saying, his arm tightening slightly around my waist. "Her office runs one of the best summer programs in DC."

"My father served on a committee with her," Williams clarified modestly. "But yes, I could make an introduction if you're interested."

"Caroline would be perfect for that," Liam said, surprising me. "Wouldn't you, sweetheart? With your interest in international policy?"

The casual endearment rolled off his tongue naturally, but the suggestion caught me off guard. Liam rarely discussed my future independently of his, and the idea that he was thinking about summer opportunities for me sent a warm flush through my chest.

"I'd be very interested," I said, meeting Williams' evaluating gaze with confidence that wasn't entirely feigned. "I've followed Senator Whitman's work on youth crisis initiatives. However, I won’t be eligible for two more years."

Williams looked impressed, and Liam's fingers pressed approvingly into my side. I'd passed some test I hadn't known I was taking.

"Let me grab us drinks," Liam said after a few more minutes of networking disguised as casual conversation. "Jack, would you excuse us?"

Williams nodded, exchanging contact information with Liam before we moved away. As we navigated through the crowd again, I felt the collective attention like a physical thing—the eyes following our progress, the subtle repositioning of bodies to intersect with our path, the performative laughter that grew louder as we approached, hoping to draw Liam's attention.

"Don’t dismiss yourself like that," Liam murmured, his lips close to my ear. "Williams was impressed. There could always be an exception, especially for you."

I smiled, accepting the compliment and the warm glow it produced. "You never mentioned Senator Whitman's program before."

"Been looking into it for you," he replied, guiding me toward the drinks table. "You'd excel there."

“But I thought Freshmen/Sophomore’s aren’t granted such opportunities.” He gave me a pointed look, “Some rules are meant to be bent and broken”
The casual way he planned my future should have bothered me more than it did. Instead, I felt a conflicted gratitude—for his belief in me, for the doors his influence could open. It was easier to focus on those aspects than to examine too closely the assumptions underneath— that my path would naturally align with his, that my success would be channeled in directions he approved.

We reached the makeshift bar, where the hockey team's most dedicated partier had appointed himself bartender. He straightened when he saw Liam, immediately reaching for the premium vodka hidden behind cheaper offerings.

"Captain! What can I get you and the lady?"

Liam ordered a whiskey for himself, then looked at me expectantly. "Just one drink for Caroline," he said, the instruction clear beneath the casual tone. "She has plans with her friends later."

"Vodka soda, please," I said, feeling Liam's approval at my choice—light, difficult to detect on my breath later, unlikely to lead to embarrassing behavior.

“Make hers weak,” Liam interjected. “Don’t need you mixing alcohol and adderall again,” he voice low so I only heard. A warning. An admonishment. Shame threatened its way up my neck, but I focused on the bartenders hand for distraction.

As we waited for our drinks, I caught sight of Chloe again, now joined by Alex. They were dancing, Chloe with enthusiastic abandon, Alex with reluctant grace. The sight of them—my roommates, my friends outside of Liam's circle—sent a pang of something like homesickness through me, though they were only yards away.

"You can join them after we make the rounds," Liam said, following my gaze. "Save me a dance.” He kissed temple.

I nodded, accepting my drink with a smile of thanks. As we turned back to the party, I caught a flash of something in Liam's eyes—a brief calculation, an assessment of the room and his position in it. Then his expression warmed as he looked down at me, and I felt myself respond to it, like a flower turning toward the sun.

"Shall we?" he asked, though it wasn't really a question.

I took a sip of my drink and nodded, sliding back into my role as effortlessly as I'd slipped into the black dress. After all, I'd been cast as the princess of this particular kingdom, and I'd finally learned to wear the crown without letting it slip.

Chloe moved like chaos contained in human form, her blonde locks catching blue and red lights as she threw her head back in laughter. Beside her, Alex swayed with precise reluctance, her movements minimal but somehow perfectly timed to the music. They were such opposites, yet they fit together in a way that made my chest ache. I watched them from across the room, suddenly aware of how much I missed them – which was ridiculous, since we shared a dorm suite and I'd seen them just hours ago. But in this moment, with the weight of Liam's hand still phantom-warm against my back, they looked like freedom.

I glanced over my shoulder, finding Liam deep in conversation with a senior whose father ran some lobbying group. His eyes flickered to me briefly, a silent permission granted with the slightest nod. Something inside me bristled at needing permission at all, but I was already moving toward my friends, drawn to their unguarded joy like a moth to flame.

"Caroline!" Chloe spotted me first, her face lighting up as she broke away from the dance floor. She launched herself at me with enough force to make me stumble backward, her arms wrapping around me in a hug that smelled like vanilla body spray and the fruity punch on her breath. "My savior! I could kiss you right now, seriously."

"What exactly did I save you from?" I laughed, steadying us both as she finally released me.

She rolled her eyes dramatically, leaning in close. "Stevenson would not take a hint. He's been following me around for twenty minutes talking about his 'hockey stats' and how he 'holds the record for most penalties in a season' like that's supposed to impress me." She mimicked a deep voice that sounded nothing like the hockey player in question. "I texted you SOS, but you must not have seen it. Then he saw you walk in with Liam and practically sprinted away. Being friends with the captain's girlfriend has its perks."

I felt a flicker of discomfort at being defined by my relationship, but Chloe's genuine relief washed it away. "Happy to intimidate by association," I said with a small smile.

Alex approached more slowly, her dark eyes taking in my appearance with the careful assessment I'd grown used to. "So this is what was in the garment bag," she said, nodding at my dress. It wasn't a question, but I heard the unspoken one beneath it.

"Ugh, no it was a god awful dress!" I confirmed, resisting the urge to explain about the emerald brocade still hanging unworn in my closet. "I told him I’d wear it for Christmas - if

he thinks I would actually wear I’m wearing a Lilly Pulitzer Reserve Collection Shift dress to a Hockey Party, he clearly hit his head a few too many times this season!"

Chloe choked on her drink with laughed, “God, and here I thought he had it all figured out.”

“But let’s be real, he was bound to mess up at least once — though I wouldn’t mentioned that to his face,” I took a sip of my weak drink.

"Well, this is very … you." Alex's words were carefully measured, her expression unreadable. But then her lips quirked slightly. "Or at least, very you these days."

The comment stung more than it should have. I opened my mouth to respond when Chloe looped her arm through mine, apparently oblivious to the tension.

"You look hot, which is what matters," she declared. "And we're celebrating tonight. No more finals, no more all-nighters, no more living in the library! Three blessed weeks of freedom!"

Her enthusiasm was infectious, and I felt the knot in my chest begin to loosen. This was what I needed – the simple pleasure of being with friends who had known me before I became an extension of Liam Barnette.

"How was your last exam?" I asked Alex, who was nursing her drink with careful sips.

"Tolerable," she replied. "Professor Harrison thinks trick questions make him interesting instead of just sadistic, but I managed."

"She's being modest," Chloe cut in. "She walked out two hours early and said, and I quote, 'If I can't solve a differential equation by now, I deserve to fail.'"

Alex shrugged, but I caught the hint of pride in her eyes. "Unlike some people, I don't need to manufacture drama about every test."

"It's not manufacturing! My econ final nearly killed me," Chloe protested. "I still have nightmares about supply curves."

She looped her arm through mine again, tugging me slightly away from Liam’s hold.

“Come to the kitchen with me. Mason’s been babysitting me all night, and I need reinforcements before Ethan starts making his usual comments.”

I let her pull me forward, but Liam’s gaze followed for just a few seconds longer.

The moment we stepped inside the kitchen, the shift in energy was immediate. Not a sudden silence, not anything dramatic, but a natural, almost unconscious shift as people acknowledged our arrival. The way conversations lifted just a little louder, the way heads turned, the way people subtly angled themselves toward us, even if they weren’t looking directly.

No one was surprised to see us. Liam was the reason this party existed. If anything, people had been waiting.

“Finally,” Mason called over the noise, a grin tugging at his lips as he cut through the crowd. A solo cup dangled from his fingers, his usual relaxed energy so ingrained in him that he could have been at any party, anywhere, and still carried the same ease. “Figured you two were too busy celebrating your 4.0s to grace us with your presence.”

“You know Liam,” Ethan drawled from behind him, his smirk already in place, the ever- present edge of amusement laced into his voice. “Has to make an entrance.”

As if summoned, Liam appeared in the doorway, casually leaning against the frame. Liam’s smirk was slower, sharper, like he already knew what game Ethan was playing. “Wouldn’t want you getting too comfortable running my house without me.”

Mason let out a laugh, clapping Liam on the shoulder. “Yeah, yeah. We managed to survive without you—barely.” His gaze flicked toward me, his smile widening. “Caroline, looking good.”

“Thanks,” I said easily, but I barely had time to respond before Chloe kept her arm looped through mine, as if to anchor me to her.

“Took you long enough,” she said, giving me a once-over that was both critical and approving. Her eyes lingered on the dress, then she exhaled through her nose, shaking her head. “Tell me Liam. Did you pick this out, too?”

“Obviously,” I said before he could answer.

Her lips twitched at the corners, caught between amusement and something else. “It’s hot. Classy, but dangerous.”

A slow, knowing smirk pulled at Liam’s mouth. “Exactly the look I was going for.” I rolled my eyes, but I couldn’t deny the way a small thrill ran through me.
The dress wasn’t meant to blend in. The fabric clung in all the right places, smooth and sleek against my skin, the hem stopping just short of inappropriate; strapless to accentuate my collarbones and bring attention to his necklace that I wore now with pride.

He didn’t dress me for this party. He dressed me for him.

Liam turned to Mason and Ethan, giving Alex, Chloe and I time to catch up.

Chloe plucked the drink from my hand, sniffing it before shooting me a look. “This is weak as hell. Who made this for you?”

“Liam,” I said with disdain.

She rolled her eyes and handed it back. “Figures. Here—” She shoved her own cup toward me. “Try mine.”

I hesitated for half a second, but Liam was already back in conversation with Mason, his attention elsewhere. I took a sip. Stronger. Warmer. The slow burn of vodka more present

than whatever watered-down version Liam had given me.

Chloe gave me a knowing look but didn’t comment. Instead, she gestured loosely to the rest of the party. “You missed pre-game. We almost had a revolt at the door. Some juniors thought they could just show up without an invite.”

I raised a brow. “They didn’t get in?”

“They wouldn’t have made it past the porch,” Mason said, shaking his head. “You know how it is. If you’re not on the list, you better have a damn good reason to be here.”

My stomach tightened slightly—not with unease, but with recognition. That had been me once. On the outside, looking in. Wondering what it took to be let in.

Now, I didn’t have to wonder.

I glanced toward Liam, but he was already ahead of me, already tuning into my thoughts before I had fully processed them myself. His fingers curled at my waist in a small, deliberate motion. A silent anchor.

That was all it took.

I exhaled, letting the weight of the thought pass, letting it settle into the past where it belonged.

Liam turned slightly toward me, his voice low. “You could’ve told them we were running late.”

Chloe smirked. “Yeah, what was the holdup?”

I shrugged, feigning indifference. “Liam was making me change.” Chloe snorted. “Yeah, that tracks.”
“Had to make sure she looked perfect,” Liam added smoothly, the ghost of a smirk at his lips. “Besides, we deserved a little celebration before coming here.”

“Correction: the originally dress he wanted me to wear was atrocious”

Liam shot me a pointed look, and I backtracked, “Great for meeting Grandma Barnette but I’m not wearing a brocade dress to a house party in December!”

Chloe threw her head back with laughter. “Hey, at least he got it right with this dress.” “It took a little coaxing,” I winked at Liam.
The words landed exactly how he wanted them to.

Chloe made a noise, rolling her eyes. “Yeah, yeah. Gross. Moving on.”

The kitchen was still packed, but it felt different than the main room—the noise was contained, the energy more focused, the alcohol flowing with practiced ease. Conversations hummed in the background, overlapping in a steady rhythm of half-finished stories and inside jokes, voices rising just enough to be heard over the music filtering in from the other side of the house.

Chloe pulled me toward the counter, her grip light but insistent, like she had been waiting for an excuse to steal me away from Liam’s orbit.

“Just one drink,” I reminded her, though I already knew it wouldn’t matter. She smirked. “Then sip mine.”
I took the cup she handed me, already warmer than I should’ve been, the heat from the packed room mixing with the slow burn of vodka settling in my stomach. I wasn’t drunk— not even close—but I felt it.

A little looser. A little less sharp around the edges.

I rolled my shoulders, exhaling as I let my gaze flick toward Liam, where he stood with Mason. Even across the room, he was magnetic, easily commanding attention even when he wasn’t speaking. He wasn’t the one telling a story, wasn’t the one making a joke, but the way people leaned in, the way they watched him for his reaction, made it obvious that his presence was the axis everything spun around.

I followed Chloe and Alex as they refilled their drinks. I offered to be taste tester, “you know, to make sure they weren’t spiked,” we joked. Between the lights, the music and the easy banter that washed over me, I felt a comforting rhythm in the midst of the chaotic symphony of the party. My attention drifted to Liam across the room, his posture relaxed yet commanding, drawing eyes like a lodestone. He laughed at something Mason said, the sound carrying across the room, making heads turn. His charisma was a force field, an invisible shield that both protected and isolated him. Ethan was leaning effortlessly against the counter top, observing, occasionally looking over our way.

"...don't you think, Caroline?" Chloe's voice pulled me back from my reverie.

"Sorry, what?" I asked, feeling the heat of a blush creep up my cheeks. I took another sip of Chloe’s drink, letting the warmth settle deeper in my chest.


She followed my gaze to Liam and rolled her eyes with mock exasperation. "Never mind, you're obviously captivated by His Highness over there."

"Just contemplating whether to rescue him from hockey talk," I deflected, though we both knew Liam needed no rescuing. He thrived on these interactions, while I merely endured them.

And then I felt it. That shift. The smallest disruption in the atmosphere, one I might not have noticed months ago, but could recognize instantly now.

Not a threat. Not urgent. But deliberate. Someone was maneuvering.

"Too late," Alex murmured, nodding towards the kitchen entrance. Ethan had arrived, flanked by a gaggle of giggling freshman girls. One in particular, tall, blonde, and radiating confidence in a crimson dress, locked eyes with Liam.

"Ah, Sloane Kensington," Chloe murmured, her tone laced with knowing amusement. "Daddy's the CEO of Merrill Lynch, Mommy's a Vanderbilt, I think - Alex, you would know better that me on that one. Just transferred from Dartmouth. Word is she's already set her sights on—"

"Liam," I finished, watching as Sloane detached herself from her group and glided towards the hockey players, her trajectory aimed directly at my boyfriend.

An uncomfortable heat bloomed in my chest as I watched her insert herself into the conversation, positioning herself front and center in Liam's line of sight. Her laugh, practiced and musical, carried across the room as she responded to something Mason said. Liam didn’t step back. He didn’t flinch. His posture remained relaxed, but I could see it—the flicker in his eyes, the subtle pause between expressions. He was registering her. Calculating. Weighing.
Cataloging.

Not her face or her body—he wasn’t that obvious.

Her last name. Her father’s board seats. Her mother’s bloodline. The connections she might offer. The social leverage she might grant. The thing I could never offer.

To anyone else, Liam looked merely polite, but I knew that look. That stillness. That exact tilt of his head when something—someone—piqued his strategic curiosity.

“She’s harmless,” Alex said lightly, misreading the sudden quiet in my body. “Liam only has eyes for you.”

It wasn’t his eyes I worried about.

It was his ambition. His relentless, methodical ambition that never missed an opportunity worth exploiting. His five-year plan. His ability to play the long game with a calm, lethal precision that made even admiration feel like a calculated move. Liam didn’t get distracted. He got interested.

And when Liam Barnette got interested in something, he never let it go without first seeing what it was worth.

Before my thoughts could spiral further, Liam's gaze found mine across the crowded room. A private smile, a subtle nod, a summons to his side. I excused myself from the girls and navigated the throng, feeling Sloane’s eyes tracking my progress.

"There she is," Mason announced as I reached them. "The brains behind the brawn. Heard you aced finals, Caroline."

"She's top of the class," Liam said before I could respond, his arm sliding possessively around my waist. "Not that anyone's surprised."

Sloane’s smile remained fixed as she extended a manicured hand. "We haven't met. I'm Sloane Kensington."

"Caroline Greer," I responded, accepting her handshake. Her grip was firm, assertive, mirroring the calculating glint in her eyes.

"Caroline's on the Marshall Scholarship short list," Liam added, his tone casual but laced with a subtle pride. "International Relations and Business double major."

Sloane’s eyes flickered with recognition. "Impressive. I'm still deciding between Political Science and Economics. Maybe you could give me some advice sometime? I hear the professors here are notoriously selective about who they mentor."

A quiet chuckle came from behind Mason, low and almost amused. Ethan.

He was watching now, lingering just outside the conversation, drink in hand, leaning against the counter as if he had no stake in the outcome—but the glint in his eyes said otherwise.

“She’s persistent,” Ethan mused, taking a sip. His tone was casual, but when his gaze slid to me, something sharp lurked beneath it. “Reminds me of someone.”

My fingers curled subtly against Liam’s jacket. I knew what he was doing. He wanted me to question if he meant Sloane—or me.

Liam didn’t give Sloane time to respond. “Caroline has her pick of mentors,” he said smoothly, his arm pulling me closer. "Professor Whitmore practically begged her to join his research team next semester — unheard of for a freshmen"

The conversation continued in this vein, a subtle power play unfolding before my eyes. Sloane, testing the waters, seeking inroads. Liam, establishing my value, marking his territory. To an outsider, he was the doting boyfriend. To me, he was a general surveying the battlefield, assessing threats and opportunities with practiced precision.

"Drink's empty," I murmured, needing a moment to breathe, to escape the suffocating atmosphere of ambition and competition.

Liam's fingers tightened briefly on my hip before releasing me. "Hurry back, Babe. I was just telling Sloane about our winter break plans."

The endearment, spoken just loud enough to carry across the room, was another territorial marker, a reminder of my place in his carefully constructed world.

At the drinks table, I took a moment to compose myself, the noise of the party receding as I focused on my breathing. I was filling a second cup when Liam appeared beside me, his

movements fluid as he took the ladle from my hand to complete the task.

"She's fishing for an invitation to Barnette’s Holiday party," he said without preamble, his voice low and conspiratorial. "I’m using you as the gateway since I wasn't taking the bait."

"Does that happen often?" I asked, striving for nonchalance.

Liam handed me my drink, his fingers brushing mine deliberately. "Girls like Sloane? All the time. They see a strategic alliance, not a person." His smile turned wry. "They don't realize I already have the perfect partnership." He gave a quick kiss to my cheek, not sure for my own benefit or to send a message.

The compliment soothed the insecurity that had begun to gnaw at the edges of my mind, even as I recognized the calculation behind it. Liam didn't waste words, especially not at a party where every interaction was a potential pawn in his intricate game.

He leaned closer, his mouth near my ear. "The difference, Caroline, is that they want what I can give them. I want what you are."

His words wrapped around me like a silken thread, beautiful and binding. Before I could respond, he was guiding me back towards our friends, his hand reclaiming its position at the small of my back.

Sloane had moved on to Ethan, but her attention remained divided, her gaze flickering towards Liam periodically. I felt her assessment each time it landed on me, measuring my worth, dissecting my vulnerabilities, searching for chinks in the armor of our relationship.

The party shifted around us as the night wore on. We migrated from kitchen to living room to the makeshift dance floor, where Liam held me closer than necessary, his hands possessive on my hips. The alcohol loosened my limbs and my tongue, though I remained mindful of my intake. Liam, ever vigilant, would intercept my hand with a glass of water when I'd had "enough."

