Dad didn’t say anything else for a while. Just kept his hands steady on the wheel, while snow flurries danced in the headlights as the road stretched endlessly ahead. Despite the heater blasting, the car retained the winter's chill, I pulled my coat closer hoping that some of Liam’s cologne lingered on it. It didn’t.

Then, more lightly: “He’s good at chess, by the way.” I glanced at him.
“We play sometimes. Through that app. He invited me to a game after Thanksgiving.” He gave a small laugh, shaking his head. “Caught me off guard—thought I’d humor him. Turns out, he’s better than I expected.”

I laughed, “Yeah, he doesn’t really do casual.”

“No, he doesn’t,” my dad agreed, glancing at me. “He’s good, but he can get a little cocky. Likes to draw things out. Makes a move, waits. Plays it slow even when he knows how it’s going to end.”

I didn’t say anything.

He smiled again, but there was something thoughtful behind it. “I’m sure you already know that about him.”

I let my shoulders sag as a chuckle escaped, “Yeah, you could say that.”

He exhaled. “He made a good impression, Caroline. He did. Don’t think that we don’t like him. Your mother’s just…” he trailed off, searching for something diplomatic. “She’s louder with her worry. That’s all.”

“What else is new.”

“But me…” he continued, his voice gentler now. “I just want to make sure you know you can come to us. That’s all this is. Not judgment. Not doubt. Just—you matter more than whoever you're with.”

I swallowed hard, staring straight ahead, heart thudding. “I know.”

He nodded again, and that time, it felt like the conversation was really over—even if it hadn’t truly begun.

The rest of drive was quiet, the silence broken only by the radio playing classic rock and the occasional swish of the windshield wipers.

“Your mother will be happy to see you,” he said as some time had passed. “She’s been planning the perfect Christmas dinner for weeks.”

I resisted the urge to scoff, the idea of a perfect anything in our family laughable. Maybe perfect in the artificial sense, but nothing authentically perfect.

“Plus your cousins will be over” that drew a genuine smile, my gaze fixed on the passing scenery, the bare trees and frost-covered fields blurring together in a monotonous landscape.

The house looked different in the glow of Christmas lights.

A wreath hung neatly on the front door, its deep green leaves dusted with frost, the red velvet bow tied to absolute perfection. White icicle lights draped over the roofline, their soft glow flickering against the snow-dusted lawn. A garland curled around the porch railing, intertwined with gold ribbon, not a single strand out of place.

Everything looked curated, staged, like something out of a holiday magazine.

We pulled into the driveway, the tires crunching over the thin layer of ice coating the pavement. My dad cut the engine, letting out a slow breath as the warmth of the car heater faded.

“Looks nice, doesn’t it?” he said, nodding toward the house. I forced a smile, shifting in my seat. “Yeah.”
It was nice.

It just didn’t feel like home.

I adjusted my coat, bracing myself for the inevitable as my dad grabbed my bags from the trunk.

Before we even made it up the front steps, the door swung open. “Caroline!”
My mother stood in the entryway, a smile stretched wide across her face, her arms open like she had been waiting there, posed perfectly for the moment.

I had seen this scene before.

The warm, practiced enthusiasm. The polished, welcoming embrace.

I stepped inside, letting her pull me into a hug that was just tight enough to feel real, just long enough to be expected.

Her hands settled against my arms as she pulled back, her eyes sweeping over me, taking stock.

“You look nice,” she said, tilting her head slightly. “More… grown up.” I blinked, caught off guard.
“College suits you.”

I wasn’t sure what to say to that.

My dad stepped past us, dropping my bags near the staircase, brushing a bit of melted snow from his coat before giving my mom a tired smile. “Long drive.”

“I can imagine,” she said, already smoothing invisible wrinkles from her sweater. “Are you hungry? I made lasagna.”

It was a test, I knew that much.

Lasagna wasn’t a traditional Christmas dish. It wasn’t something she made often. But it was my favorite when I was younger—before I started worrying about calories, before I started skipping meals in high school, before I had learned that food was another thing to control.

“Yeah,” I said finally. “That sounds great.”

Her eyes flickered, just briefly, before she nodded, satisfied. “Good. Come get settled before dinner.”

I followed her inside, the warmth of the house wrapping around me, the faint scent of pine and cinnamon filling the air.

Everything looked pristine, picture-perfect, arranged exactly the way she wanted.

The stockings hung evenly along the fireplace. The tree was tall, symmetrical, decorated in gold and white—not a single homemade ornament, not a single thing that wasn’t carefully selected. Holiday cards lined the mantel, a collection of handwritten well-wishes from family friends, colleagues, people my mother would never let herself fall out of touch with.

It was beautiful.

And I felt like a visitor.

I took the stairs slowly, trailing my fingers along the polished wooden railing as I made my way to my bedroom.

My bags sat neatly by the door, but nothing else had changed.

The bed was still made the way I had left it at Thanksgiving, the decorative pillows arranged perfectly.

Framed photos sat on my desk, frozen moments of a past life—high school dances, summer vacations, pictures of me with childhood friends who had since moved away.

I swallowed, stepping further inside, running my hand over the comforter. It felt foreign.
Like I was walking through an old version of myself, someone I had already outgrown. And maybe I had.
I pulled my phone from my pocket, my fingers moving automatically to send a message.

Caroline: I’m here.

I hesitated for only a second before hitting send. A response came immediately.
Liam: FaceTime me.

I exhaled, sinking onto the edge of my bed, already pressing the call button. The screen flickered, and then there he was.
Liam’s face came into focus—sharp jaw, dark brows, that knowing smirk that told me he had been waiting for this. He was leaning against the headboard of his bed, hair still damp from a shower, a gray hoodie slouched over his broad shoulders.

“About time,” he drawled, his voice settling over me like something tangible. “I was starting to think you forgot about me.”

I rolled my eyes, sinking back against my pillows. “Yeah, because you’re so easy to forget.”

“So this is it,” he said, leaning back in his chair, arms folding lazily over his chest. “The infamous childhood bedroom.”

I exhaled, adjusting the camera slightly. “Happy now?” “You have no idea.”
He tilted his head, gaze flickering over the details behind me—the neatly made bed, the color-coordinated bookshelf, the framed photos from high school dances and family vacations that felt like a lifetime ago.

“Cute,” he mused. “Predictable, but cute.” I rolled my eyes. “Are you done?”
“Not even close. What about the awards?” he asked, nodding toward the polished plaques lined up on my shelves.

I hesitated before turning the camera back, showing the collection—honor roll recognitions, debate team wins, academic awards, all neatly arranged like proof of something.

“Overachiever,” Liam murmured, but there was pride in his voice. “You already knew that.”
“Yeah,” he said, watching me through the screen. “But it’s different seeing it like this.”

He squinted slightly, like he was analyzing a crime scene. “Is that a ‘Future Leaders of America’ plaque?”

I bit the inside of my cheek. “Don’t start.”

“And… Junior Statemen of America? Jesus, Greer. You really were insufferable.” “I was ambitious.”
“You still are.” His voice was softer now, more thoughtful, but the teasing edge never fully left.

His gaze shifted slightly, landing on the corkboard pinned to my wall—cluttered with old ticket stubs, competition ribbons, handwritten notes and Polaroids from friends I haven’t talked to in months.

Liam’s smirk faltered, just a fraction. “You kept all that?”
I hesitated. “I guess.”

After a beat, I jabbed. “Getting sentimental, Barnette?”

He exhaled a short laugh. “Not a chance. Let’s see the bookcase”

He studied it for a moment longer, then hummed, shifting back slightly.

“Color-coded bookshelves, Caroline?” His voice was laced with amusement. “Are they arranged alphabetically, too?”

“No.” I hesitated. “By genre.”

“It makes no sense,” he countered, shaking his head. “What happens if you forget what color a book is?”

“Then I remember. My system works.” “Your system is psychotic.”
“Says the man who alphabetizes his liquor cabinet.” His smirk widened. “That’s just efficiency.”
I rolled my eyes, but he wasn’t done.

I adjusted the phone again, this time angling it back toward me. “Well, in a few days, it’s my turn. Hope your childhood bedroom is ready for critique.”

Liam grinned, eyes dark with amusement. “We’ll see if you can handle it.” “Oh, I can handle it,” I assured him.
“We’ll see about that, sweetheart.”

The call stretched on—his voice filling the space, easing the weight of being home. And for a moment, it didn’t matter that I was here and he was there.

Because we were still us.

Only my mom calling up that dinner was ready got us off the phone. As I hung up, I wonder how many other times she would interrupt us over the next few days.


Dinner started normally.

The clink of silverware against plates, the occasional scrape of a chair as someone adjusted their posture, the low hum of Christmas music filtering in from the living room. The house smelled like garlic, fresh basil, a hint of wine from my mother’s glass.

It was comfortable, or at least, it could have been.

“Did you see that article I sent you?” my dad asked, reaching for the parmesan.

“The one about the Supreme Court?” I speared a piece of lasagna, nodding. “Yeah, I read it.” “What do you think?”
“I think their interpretation of the Commerce Clause is way too broad, but that’s nothing new.”

His mouth twitched into something close to a smile. “That’s my girl.” Warmth curled in my chest—brief, fleeting.
“Did you see the one about the new AI regulations?” I asked. “Not yet. I’ll read it later.”
“You’ll like it,” I said, shifting slightly in my seat. “It ties into the antitrust discussions from last month.”

“Good. We’ll talk about it when I do.”

It was familiar, easy—a shared language, one we both understood. And then—
“Oh, Caroline, I meant to tell you—” my mother cut in smoothly, as if she had just remembered something offhand. “I ran into Mrs. Quinn the other day. Do you remember her son Tyler?”

My stomach tensed. “Yeah,” I said carefully.
“Well, he’s doing really well. Business major, already interning at his uncle’s firm. His girlfriend seems absolutely lovely. You should meet her.”

I forced a small, neutral smile. “That’s great for him.” I did not acknowledge the second part.
“You two dated for a while, didn’t you?”

“Yeah, for a bit,” I said, my voice light, detached. “It wasn’t serious.”

We only dated for 2 years and lost my virginity to him, but no definitely not serious, I thought to myself.

“Oh, I don’t know,” she mused, turning the stem of her wine glass between her fingers. “You were practically inseparable back then.”

“Mom,” I said, keeping my voice even, unaffected. “That was ages ago.”

“Mmm.”

It was a non-answer, a quiet dismissal.

I took a sip of water, swallowing down the irritation creeping up my spine. My mother’s gaze flickered toward my necklace.
“That’s nice,” she said, tilting her head slightly. “Is it new?”

“Yeah,” I said, knowing exactly where this was going. “Liam bought it for me. I thought I was wearing it when I came down for Thanksgiving?”

She hummed, taking another slow sip of wine. “I hope you’re not using him for his money.” I froze—just for a second.
Not enough for my dad to notice. But she did.

I let out a small breath of laughter, shaking my head. “That’s funny.” “I wasn’t joking.”
“Neither was I.”

I reached for my water, keeping my expression blank.

“I just mean,” she continued, her voice pleasant, casual, “it’s easy to get swept up when someone’s spoiling you.”

“Liam doesn’t spoil me,” I said, setting my glass down with deliberate care. “He’s thoughtful. There’s a difference.”

“Mmm.”

The sound was nothing and everything all at once.

I could feel my dad shifting beside me, choosing not to engage.

She let the silence hang, then spoke with offhand ease, as if changing the subject.

“I had lunch with Dana Collier last week. You remember her? Her daughter Claire started at MIT this year—biochem and engineering, double major. She’s already doing research with a professor and tutoring.”

She didn’t look at me. Just took another sip of wine and added, “Some girls just know how to stay focused.”

My stomach tightened, but I didn’t flinch.

“Sounds like she’s doing great,” I said evenly.

My mother gave a small smile, as if pleased we agreed. “She is.”

The implication hung between us like smoke—Caroline could be, too, if she weren’t wasting time playing house with an older boyfriend.

My dad cleared his throat. “Caroline’s at the top of her class.”

“I know,” my mother replied, voice light. “I’m not criticizing. I’m just saying—freshman year sets the tone.”

She said it like a compliment. It didn’t land like one.

Then, as if sensing she'd pushed far enough, she shifted her tone, lightening it. “How’s Chloe doing? I haven’t seen her in ages. Still Communications?”

I blinked. “Yeah. Focusing on Public Relations. Still Chloe.”

My mother laughed—gently this time. “She always did have too much energy. Like she was sprinting through life in heels.”

I smiled despite myself. “She hasn’t changed.”

“And Liam?” she asked, more carefully now. “How’s he handling the semester?”

I studied her face, looking for the edge. But there wasn’t one—not this time. Just curiosity, maybe even something close to interest.

“He’s good,” I said. “Busy. Focused. Hockey, Academics, any spare time, he’s working on projects for that internship”

She nodded, tracing the rim of her wine glass. “He seems very grounded.”

And for the first time that night, the silence that followed didn’t sting. It settled—tentative, but not sharp. A ceasefire, for now.

Then, lightly, “Are you ready to meet his parents?”

I glanced up. “They invited me to dinner in January. In Boston.”

“That’s a big deal,” she said, folding her napkin. “His father being a senator and all.” Her tone wasn’t accusatory. If anything, it was almost—curious.
“You’ll remember your manners, of course. And those etiquette classes I made you take.” I nearly choked on my water.

Two summers. Twelve years old. Weekly three-hour sessions in a stuffy garden room with folding chairs, posture drills, white gloves, and endless talk about salad forks and thank-you notes. One of the girls cried during the formal tea service. I envied her.

“Hard to forget,” I said mildly.

My mother gave a knowing little smile. “You can’t re-do a first impression. I’m sure Liam knows that. And I’m sure you don’t want to embarrass him… or us.”

There it was. A velvet glove, but the pressure underneath was familiar. I nodded once. “I won’t.”
“I know,” she said.

And this time, I almost believed her.


Later, after the dishes were stacked in the sink and my dad had retreated upstairs with the paper tucked under his arm, I found myself alone with my mother in the family room. The lights were dim, the fire low. She sat on the edge of the sofa like she was waiting for a cue that only she could hear.

I didn’t sit. I leaned against the armrest across from her, arms folded. She looked up from her wine. “I think we should have the talk again.”
I let my head fall back with a groan. “Mom. I’m nineteen. I know how sex works.”

“I know you do.” Her tone didn’t shift. She might as well have been commenting on the weather. “This isn’t about biology. It’s about dignity.”

I blinked at her.

She set her glass down with care, fingers grazing the rim. “It’s a keep your legs shut
conversation.”

There it was—cool, blunt, sharpened by decades of performance.

“You were raised a certain way, Caroline. With values. Standards. I know how tempting it can be, especially with someone like Liam—older, polished, generous. And I’m not saying he’s not a good man. I’m saying good men still lose respect for girls who give too much of themselves too soon.”

I didn’t answer. I didn’t have to. She kept going.

“You can love someone, want someone, and still ruin the entire thing by making yourself too easy. You have no idea how quickly boys change once they’ve had what they wanted.”

I crossed my arms tighter, jaw clenched.

“If Liam is a good guy, the kind of man you think he is, he’ll understand that you’re worth waiting for. And if he doesn’t—” she gave a tight, practiced shrug, “then you’ll know exactly what kind of man he really is.”

I looked at her then. Really looked.

And in that moment, I understood: this wasn’t about Liam. It wasn’t even about me. It was about image. About reputation – hers more so than mine. It was about keeping the illusion polished from the outside, even if everything inside had already cracked.

“I appreciate the advice,” I said quietly, voice flat.

She studied me for a moment, trying to decide if I meant it. Then she smiled—cool and satisfied, like she’d done her part.

“I’m your mother. It’s my job.”

She said it like that meant something. Like it absolved her.

I didn’t answer. Just watched the way the firelight caught the rim of her glass, the gleam of her ring, the practiced ease in her posture—as if she truly believed the world still worked the way it did when she was nineteen.

And maybe for her, it had. But not for me.
She was out of touch. Completely, painfully out of touch. And I hated how much that still disappointed me.

Sometimes I wished she were the kind of mother I could talk to—really talk to. The kind who wouldn’t flinch or recoil if I told her the truth. But I knew better. She wouldn’t respect me if she knew. She wouldn’t like me.

If she was this far removed from what it meant to be young and trying to navigate all of this
—love, sex, autonomy—how could I trust her with anything else? With anxiety, or ambition, or the truth about what Liam and I were?

I couldn't.

And somehow, that stung more than the words she'd actually said.



Chapter 40.



The house smelled like cinnamon, fresh coffee, and maple syrup—the familiar scent of Christmas morning, warm and nostalgic. My dad was at the stove, flipping pancakes the way he had every year since I was a kid, humming along to the soft notes of Bing Crosby playing from the living room.

This was what I had always loved about Christmas.

The quiet slowness of the morning. The way the fireplace crackled softly. The way the world outside felt untouched, blanketed in fresh snow, while inside, everything was warm and familiar.

This year, the only thing missing was Liam. I handed my parents their gifts, watching as they unwrapped them with a mix of curiosity and hesitation.

Two ceramic mugs—one reading “Holbrook University Mom” and the other “ Holbrook University Name Dad.” Simple, thoughtful. Something they could actually use.

My dad let out a small chuckle, turning his over in his hands. “Well, I guess I have no excuse not to drink my coffee out of this now.”

“Exactly,” I said, allowing myself a small smile.

For him, I had also picked out a book on presidential administrations and their impact on the space program over the years—something I knew he’d actually read. His brows lifted slightly when he saw the title, intrigued.

“You picked a good one,” he said, nodding approvingly.

My mother carefully lifted the lid on a velvet jewelry box, revealing a delicate silver bracelet with a dainty charm of her birthstone and mine. I had picked out for her while I was out shopping with Chloe one day. Understated, elegant. Something I thought she would like.

Her lips pressed together, eyes scanning the bracelet like she was trying to force herself to appreciate it.

“Caroline, this is too much,” she said, her voice gentle, but edged with something else. “You’re in college. You can’t afford to buy us things like this.”

“It wasn’t that expensive,” I said quickly.

Her fingers brushed over the metal, but she didn’t try it on. “I just don’t know if I pull off silver.”

I swallowed, forcing a smile. “You don’t have to wear it if you don’t like it.”

“It’s lovely,” she said, closing the box. A finality in the gesture. “It’s just—you shouldn’t be spending money like this.” I nodded, saying nothing.

Then came my turn.

A neatly wrapped box, crisp edges, carefully tied ribbon. My mother’s doing.

I peeled back the paper, the soft crinkle of wrapping giving way to a fabric book bag, colorful and quilted, the kind of thing I would have carried everywhere in high school.

The brand was nice. Expensive in the way that signaled practicality rather than luxury. I smoothed my hand over the fabric, the bright floral pattern almost jarring.

“I figured you could use something for all those heavy books you’re always lugging around,” my mom said lightly.

She wasn’t wrong. I did carry my books everywhere.

But now, I carried them in a structured leather tote, sleek and understated, something that felt like me.

This… wasn’t me. Not anymore.

“It’s perfect,” I said, schooling my features into a polite smile.

“I thought it was fun,” my mom continued, watching me carefully. “I remember you used to love these.”

Used to. I swallowed down the strange, hollow feeling in my chest. She wasn’t wrong. I had loved them—back when I was softer, when I wasn’t as put together, when I still felt comfortable in things that were casual and familiar.

“Thank you. It’s really beautiful” I said, keeping my voice even. She smiled, satisfied. Not warm, not affectionate. Just… pleased. And maybe that should have been enough.
I blinked back the realization that even after all this time, they hadn’t noticed how much I’d changed.

Or maybe, they just didn’t want to.

My dad stretched, setting his mug down.

“We should all start getting ready,” he said, glancing at the clock. “Company will be here before we know it.”

My mother hummed in agreement, already moving to clear the wrapping paper. Christmas morning was over.
And I wasn’t sure how I felt about it.


The house had settled into a temporary quiet, the aftermath of unwrapping gifts leaving stray pieces of wrapping paper on the floor and half-empty coffee cups on the table. My dad had retreated to the kitchen, rinsing dishes, while my mom busied herself with tidying up, as if the house had to be spotless before company arrived.

I took this opportunity to rummage through the hallway closet. It wasn’t impulsive. It wasn’t emotional. It was strategic. Just one to keep in my pocket – just in case.

The Xanax was exactly where it had always been. Buried in the same travel case my mom brought to Cape May every summer, the label worn smooth. I popped the pill from its foil backing with more precision than I wanted to admit, wrapped it in a napkin and tucked it in my pocket, lying to myself I would only take it if I really needed a break.

I pulled out my phone, walking towards my room for a moment of privacy as I tapped Liam’s contact. I could easily change and chat with my boyfriend, I reasoned.

He picked up on the second ring, his voice warm, familiar. “Merry Christmas, sweetheart.”

A breath I hadn’t realized I was holding released at the sound of his voice. “Merry Christmas.”

I could hear the murmur of conversation in the background—his family, probably gathered in the grand estate that I would be stepping into soon enough.

“How’s your morning?” he asked.

“It’s fine. The usual.” I responded as I walked into my room, shutting the door. “Dad loved the book on presidential history.”

“Not surprised,” Liam said approvingly. “What about your mom?” I hesitated. “Eh, the bracelet came with some commentary.”
He was silent for a moment, long enough to say everything without saying anything at all. “She didn’t like it?”

“She said it wasn’t her color.”

Liam made a low sound in his throat. “She’s impossible.”

I sighed, tilting my head back against my door. “She means well.” “Sure,” he said, unconvinced.
I shook my head, pushing the thought away. “What about you? What did you get?”

“The usual,” he said, sounding amused. “New watch, custom cufflinks, things I’ll probably never wear.”

I smiled. “Rich people gifts.” “Exactly.”
“My mom gave me the sex talk,” I said, flopping onto my bed with a dramatic sigh. I laughed, mostly to shake off the memory. It still made my skin crawl.
There was a beat of silence. Then Liam, dry as ever:
“She knows we’ve already crossed that bridge, right?”

“Apparently not. Or she’s pretending. Honestly, I can’t tell which is worse.” “Go on.”
“It was the full Catholic monologue—values, temptation, how I was ‘raised a certain way.’ And then the pièce de résistance: ‘This is a keep your legs shut conversation.’”

He let out a low whistle. “Ah, the classics. Did she hand you a purity ring or just guilt and judgment?”

“Just judgment. But it was monogrammed.”

He laughed, warm and amused. “And here I was worried about impressing her.” “You’re already ahead of the curve. They don’t know you ruined me yet.” “Yet,” he echoed. “Good to know expectations are high.”
I smiled, curling onto my side. “She said if you’re really a good guy, you’ll understand waiting.”

“Oh, I understand waiting,” Liam said lightly. “I’m just not interested in pretending we’re still in a Jane Austen novel.”

I huffed a laugh into my pillow. “You’d be a terrible Mr. Darcy.” “I’d be banned from the ballroom in under five minutes.”

“With cause.”

Another beat passed, easier now.

“She also asked if I’m ready to meet your parents,” I said, softer. “And are you?”
I hesitated, then nodded, even though he couldn’t see it. “I think so.”

“You’ll be fine,” he said. “Just don’t mention your mother’s motivational speeches. My dad might ask for a transcript.”

That made me laugh again—louder this time. “God, can you imagine?” “I can. And I wish I couldn’t.”
Before I could respond, the sound of the doorbell rang through the house, signaling the arrival of family, and my stomach clenched.

I straightened, inhaling sharply. “I have to go.” “Call me later,” Liam said, his voice softer now. “I will.”
“Merry Christmas, Sweetheart.”

My fingers curled around the phone, as if I could hold onto him through the speaker. “Merry Christmas, baby.”

I inhaled, bracing myself, then moved toward the front door.

The second I opened it, a gust of cold air swept in, along with the chaotic energy of my cousins. The chill bit at my skin, sharp and bracing, a stark contrast to the warmth of the house. I could already hear the shouts and laughter, the sound of running feet crunching over snow as they raced up the walkway. There was no mistaking them for anyone else. They were loud, exuberant, and completely themselves—like a force of nature that swept everything else away in its path. I stepped back, half-stunned by the sudden whirlwind of noise and cold.

“Merry Christmas!” Ava announced, shoving past me with an armful of gifts, her boots clunking against the hardwood as she kicked them off mid-step.

Maddie trailed right behind her, already unwinding her scarf. “Caroline, I need you to back me up—Mom is being insane and saying I can’t go to New York for New Year’s.”

“That’s because you’re 14 and would follow a stranger into his van on the promise of Taylor Swift tickets,” Ben muttered as he followed behind them, dropping a duffel bag at the door

and giving me a one-armed hug in passing, like it was an afterthought.

“Merry Christmas to you too,” I said dryly, shutting the door as my aunts and uncles stepped inside with a flurry of movement—coats being peeled off, greetings being exchanged.

The house was instantly louder, instantly fuller. Noise and energy swirled around me as I tried to absorb the sudden change in atmosphere. My cousins’ laughter echoed off the walls, and the air was tinged with the crisp scent of winter that clung to their coats. I made the rounds, hugging my aunts, exchanging pleasantries with my uncles, doing my best to keep up with the happy chaos that filled the front hall.

Ava and Maddie were already shouting over each other, voices overlapping in a familiar, insistent rhythm. Ben trailed behind, grinning as he watched his sister’s and cousin’s antics, clearly amused by their single-mindedness in trying to recruit me to their cause. I was about to respond, to offer some reassurance that I would indeed back them up, when my mother swept in, her presence commanding and efficient.

She moved swiftly, already directing traffic with her usual practiced warmth, ushering people toward the living room like it was a choreographed performance. The adults followed, laughing and chatting, comfortable with the roles we all seemed to fall into every year.

I was about to follow when my mother paused, giving me a look that signaled she had plans for me, plans that did not involve joining the adults.

“Caroline, take the kids downstairs,” she said, giving me a polite smile, which meant I was now in charge of keeping them entertained. Not that they needed me to.

Then, casually, almost as an afterthought, she added toward her sister with a soft, practiced laugh, “She’s always been better with teenagers than adults—less pressure to hold a real conversation.”

It landed with a smile, as all her sharpest jabs did.

I grabbed a stack of solo cups and smuggled a pint of whiskey under my sweater and headed downstairs. By the time I reached the basement, they had already taken over: Ava was sprawled on the couch, aux cord in hand, the speakers already blasting early 2000s throwbacks. Maddie was mid-TikTok dance in front of the TV. Ben sat in the armchair, arms crossed, looking like he was questioning every life choice that had led him to this moment.

I sighed, leaning against the wall. “You’ve been here for five minutes and already taken over.”

I poured myself a jack & coke and offered solo cups and soda to my cousins. “Exactly,” Ava said, grinning as she turned the music up.
Ben shot me a long-suffering look. “Save me.”

“You’re on your own.” Strategically, I swallowed the Xanax with my drink and waited for its effects.

Soon, Christmas didn’t feel so heavy anymore.

“This is a crime against music,” Ben announced, arms crossed as Ava blasted early 2000s throwbacks through the speakers.

“You’re just mad because you know all the words,” Ava shot back, grinning as she turned the volume up.

“Of course I do,” Ben muttered. “I’ve been subjected to your terrible taste for years.”

I leaned back into the couch, pulling my knees up, watching them bicker with the kind of fondness that only came from years of growing up together.

“Caroline, you have to be on my side,” Ben said, turning to me. “Tell her that her playlist is trash.”

