I tried to sit up straighter, but the room tilted slightly. "You promised me."
My throat closed. I couldn’t speak. I just nodded. Guilt bloomed thick in my chest—sour and cloying, curling beneath my ribs like smoke I couldn’t exhale.

Ava helped me to my feet, her hand warm and grounding on my back. The world had gone cotton-soft, my limbs heavy. My cousins guided me up the stairs like I was fragile and breakable.

Once upstairs, I sank into the bed. Ava fluffed my pillow, set a glass of water on the nightstand.

She sat with me for a few minutes, murmuring reassurances Liam fed through the phone. I could hear his voice—low, measured, trying to keep me present.

