I lay there, eyes fluttering closed, the room spinning gently. I reached for the phone, still open on the bed beside me.

"Caroline." Liam’s voice was gentler now, quieter. "I’m okay," I whispered. "I’m just tired."
"You shouldn’t have done this." "I know."
He was silent for a beat. Then: "I’ll stay on the line until you fall asleep."

Before I could respond, the door opened again—this time with more purpose. My mother stepped in, still in heels, still with a wine glass pinched between her fingers.

Her eyes swept the room, landing on me in bed.

“You’re in bed?” Her voice was already edged with disbelief. “Are you sick?”

“No,” I said quickly, pushing myself up against the headboard. “Just a headache. Ava brought me water. It’s not a big deal.”

She took a step forward, narrowing her gaze. “You’re pale. Are you—” Her eyes flicked to my stomach. “Are you pregnant?”

