And then she spotted the phone still beside me—screen still softly lit, FaceTime still open. Her expression twisted.
“Oh. I see.” Her voice sharpened like broken glass. “You’re not sick. You just wanted to sneak away and talk to your boyfriend.”

I opened my mouth, but nothing came out.

“It’s Christmas, Caroline. Your entire family is downstairs, and you’re up here whispering to Liam like a love-struck teenager?”

“I wasn’t—”

Liam’s voice came through the phone, calm but direct. “Mrs. Greer, I’m sorry. That’s my fault.”

Her eyes snapped to the device like it had betrayed her personally.

“I asked her to stay on the line,” he continued, measured. “I needed an escape from my brother. That’s on me. I thought company had already left. I didn’t mean to keep Caroline. It won’t happen again.”

She laughed—short and biting. “It won’t, because I’m taking the phone.”

Before I could react, she plucked it from the blanket and pressed the red button to end the call.

“She’ll call you when company’s gone,” she said crisply, placing the phone in her pocket. “You’re done for tonight.”

“Mom—”

“Get out of bed. If you’re well enough to flirt, you’re well enough to be with your family.”

My mouth was dry as I swung my legs over the edge of the bed, clutching the water like it might anchor me to the floor. My limbs felt too long and too soft, as if gravity had suddenly become selective.

My mother stood by the door, waiting. Watching. I didn’t meet her eyes as I padded past her in my socks.

We walked the hallway in silence—at least until the staircase came into view. Then she started again.

“This is exactly what I mean when I say you’re slipping,” she murmured, voice low and sharp enough to cut. “Hiding upstairs like a child. Crying to Liam. Honestly, Caroline. If you

can’t hold yourself together over the holidays, how are you going to survive in the real world?”

I bit the inside of my cheek, the water glass trembling slightly in my grip.

She continued, a little too pleased with herself now. “You’ve embarrassed yourself. And me. Don’t think the aunts haven’t noticed. You were always the one we could count on to perform.”

The bottom step creaked beneath my foot.

And then—like a light switch flipping—her voice changed.

“I found her!” she said brightly, stepping into the living room with a politician’s smile. “Just a headache – maybe too much wine with dinner. But she insisted she’s already feeling better. Go on back down with the cousins. They’ve missed you.”

I nodded mutely, eyes fixed on the floor, and turned toward the basement stairs without another word.

Behind me, the hum of holiday conversation rose again, cheerful and oblivious. And I descended. Back into the noise. Back into the warmth. Back into the act.
“Oh thank God, you’re back!” Ben said as if I had left him in a room with religious fanatics trying to convert him.

