“Well given your state, I think we should take advantage and see how much information we can get from you.” Ben grinned mischievously.

“Do you really want to go there, Benjamin? We all know Ava and I have a full dossier on you that we would hate to have to share with the class.”

“Who said it had to be nefarious, maybe I just want to know innocent gossip” “Innocent and gossip are two words that don’t go together”
“Caroline, be so honest right now,” Maddie said, flipping onto her stomach and propping herself up on her elbows. “Have you sent him nudes?”

“Oh my God, I am not answering that!” I threw a pillow at her, which she caught easily. “That’s a yes,” Ava said, nodding sagely.
“That is not a yes!”

I let out a long, dramatic sigh, sinking further into the couch. “Fine. If you want drama, Liam got into a fight over me during one of his games.”

That got their attention.

Ava’s head snapped up from her phone. Maddie, who had been tossing a stress ball in the air, fumbled and dropped it. Ben, who had been half-listening at best, suddenly looked intrigued.

“What?” Ava demanded, sitting up straighter. “He what?” Maddie practically shrieked.
Ben groaned, rubbing his hands over his face. “Of course, he did.”

“Explain,” Ava said, leaning forward like she was about to be served the gossip of the year.

I hesitated for half a second. Then, realizing there was no getting out of it, I waved a hand vaguely. “It was pretty intense. He got suspended for two games.”

Maddie gasped. “Oh my God. What did the guy say?” “I don’t know,” I said quickly. Too quickly.
Ava squinted. “Caroline.” “I really don’t!” I insisted.
“But he got suspended,” Maddie pressed. “That means it had to be something serious.”

“It was probably just hockey stuff,” I muttered, knowing damn well it wasn’t just hockey stuff.

“Yeah, because guys just randomly throw punches for no reason,” Ava said dryly.

“In hockey? Yeah, that’s like 30% of the game.” Maddie pointed out. “He’s the captain? So what, he’s been in a dozen or so fights. I highly doubt this was just some routine hockey fight.” I sometimes forgot how Maddie was getting older and wiser while I had been away.

Ben sighed heavily, shaking his head. “Great. Just what we need. A guy with a hero complex.”

“Liam does not have a hero complex,” I said. Liam absolutely had a hero complex. The kind that came with tailored coats, contingency plans, and the belief that if he didn’t personally prevent my collapse, no one would, but I wasn’t about to admit that out loud.

“Caroline,” Ava said, deadpan. “He got suspended.”

“That just means he’s bad at controlling his temper,” Ben muttered.

“Okay, first of all,” I said, snatching a pillow and throwing it at him. “You grilled him at Thanksgiving, if I recall, he passed the sniff test then. What else do you want from him? His

blood type?”

“Wouldn’t hurt,” Ben said with a shrug.

“You know, just in case we need it for future reference,” Ava added.

Maddie nodded solemnly. “You’re in a serious relationship now, Caroline. We’re just being responsible.”

I groaned, dramatically, dragging my hands down my face. “You three are impossible”

“Am I the only one with a love life?” I demanded, narrowing my eyes at them. “Ava? Ben? Seeing anyone?”

Ben made a disgusted face. “I reject the concept of romance entirely.” “That just means he’s still in love with his ex,” Ava muttered.
“I am not!”

“Ben, it’s fine,” Maddie said soothingly. “Denial is part of the process.”

“I hate all of you,” he said, grabbing a couch pillow and smashing it over his face. “Ava?” I prodded.
She shrugged, flipping through her phone. “No one worth mentioning. You know how men are.”

“No, please, enlighten me,” I said dryly. “Disappointing,” she replied simply.
“Useless,” Maddie sighed, dramatically collapsing back onto the floor. “Entirely,” Ava agreed.
Ben just exhaled loudly. “You guys are exhausting.” I sat back, shaking my head, but smiling.
“Okay, enough talking,” I said, snatching a pillow and tossing it at Maddie’s head. “Let’s do something fun before I’m forced to make small talk with extended family.”

“We could play Mario Kart,” Ben suggested, already reaching for the controllers.

“No, because I don’t feel like watching you throw a tantrum when you lose,” Ava said. “Excuse me,” Ben scoffed. “I am a very gracious winner.”
“Right, but you’re not a winner, so that doesn’t apply.”

Ben grabbed another couch pillow and chucked it at her. Ava dodged effortlessly.

“What about Cards Against Humanity?” Maddie suggested, sitting up. “I feel like we haven’t played that in forever.”

“That’s because last time, Ben nearly died when my mom read one of the cards out loud,” I reminded her.

Ben shuddered violently. “We are never speaking of that again.” “Fine, fine,” Maddie sighed. “Then poker?”
“Do you even know how to play poker?” I asked, raising an eyebrow. “No,” she admitted, grinning. “But I can learn.”
“We could bet candy,” Ava suggested.

“I was thinking actual money,” Maddie countered.

“Great, another thing I can destroy you all at,” Ben said smugly.

