“I just don’t give everything away on my face like you guys do.” “Exactly,” Ava said. “Terrifying.”
I rolled my eyes but grabbed the deck of cards off the coffee table anyway. “Fine. Let’s play.” A half-hour later, Ava was out, Maddie was down to her last few candies, Ben was still bluffing like an idiot, and I was comfortably in control of the game.

“I don’t understand,” Maddie said, staring at me like I had just performed witchcraft. “How are you doing this?”

“I told you,” Ava said, reclining dramatically on the couch. “She’s a menace.” “I want a rematch,” Ben grumbled, slumping in defeat.
“Of course you do,” I said sweetly. “One more round,” Maddie pleaded.

I sighed, but I was laughing. “Fine. But after this, we switch to Mario Kart so I can destroy Ben in something else.”

Ben groaned. Ava smirked. Maddie cheered.

And just like that, Christmas didn’t feel heavy anymore. Just fun. Just home.


As the evening came to an end, there were heartfelt embraces, drawn-out farewells, and assurances to reunite soon. Maddie insisted Ava promise to visit before summer, while Ben muttered about needing "at least six months to emotionally recover from this chaos." Their parents eventually guided them toward the exit, hurriedly putting on their coats as they stepped into the chilly night. The house felt noticeably quieter in their absence, leaving behind the kind of warmth that gradually fades after a busy day.

Cleaning up was quieter than usual. My dad rinsed dessert plates in the sink while my mom lit another candle like we hadn’t fought upstairs, her voice syrupy sweet as she asked Aunt Lisa about her new kitchen backsplash. I helped pack leftovers into plastic containers, head still heavy, limbs a beat behind my brain.

No one said anything about me disappearing. Not even Maddie.

Ava slipped my phone back to me sometime after the dishes were done—tucked into my palm with a look that said don’t worry, I didn’t tell him everything. I didn’t ask how she got it. I didn’t have to.

There were already texts waiting.

BEN: yo Liam is chill but terrifying when he’s disappointed just FYI AVA: he’s been texting me every hour to check if you’re awake LIAM: Let me know when you’re alone. Or when she’s gone. I’ll wait.

It was past midnight before I got upstairs. The house had quieted. Even the hallway lights had been dimmed, and the guest bedrooms had stopped murmuring.

I climbed into bed, curled beneath the throw blanket that still smelled like peppermint and wood smoke, and texted him a single word: Alone.

The call came through five seconds later.

“I’m okay,” I murmured, though the words tasted false—like something I was trying to convince myself of more than him.

“You’re not,” he said, evenly. “And that’s exactly why I didn’t want you there by yourself.”

His voice was quiet, deliberate—unshaken. Not angry. Worse. He sounded certain. Like tonight had just confirmed a theory he’d always held.

“You think you’re in control until you’re not. And then what? Your little cousin answers the phone, and you’re slurring and too out of it to form a sentence?”

I flinched, even though he hadn’t raised his voice. He never needed to.

“This is why I told you not to go without me - not to be some controlling asshole but because I knew it was too soon for you to be under that roof without someone to keep you safe.”

I turned onto my side, biting back the urge to protest. I’d made the choice. Taken the pill. Poured the drink. Ignored the plan. There was nothing left to argue.

“Cue the cape,” I muttered. “The savior complex is alive and well.” There was a pause—long enough to sting.
Then, colder: “We’ll talk in the car tomorrow.” I waited.
“I’m just glad I was already planning to pick you up,” he added, clipped. “Because if I weren’t, this wouldn’t have end with a phone call. I’d be on your doorstep.”

I shut my eyes.

“I guess it’s fitting,” I said softly. “Ending the night disappointing everyone.” Silence. Then:
“No, Caroline,” he said flatly. “You don’t get to retreat into that.” His tone wasn’t cruel. Just absolute.
“You messed up. I’ll handle it. But don’t pretend it didn’t matter. And don’t pretend it didn’t prove my point.”

I didn’t answer.

“You want to be trusted? Start acting like it. Tomorrow, we reset. But this conversation isn't over.”

I swallowed hard. There it was. The boundary. The judgment. The promise of correction— just delayed.

And somehow, that made it easier to breathe. Tomorrow, I’d be in his car. Under his rules. His control.

And maybe that was the part that hurt the most— That after everything, I wanted him in charge.
Because his control didn’t just suffocate.

It steadied.

And in the wreckage of my own making, it was the only thing that felt like love.

Chapter 41.



The house smelled like coffee and pine, the last traces of Christmas lingering in the air. My bags were packed, lined up neatly by the door, along with hostess gifts for the Barnettes. I had double-checked. Triple-checked. There was nothing left to do except leave.

Liam’s car pulled into the driveway, the sound of tires crunching over the thin layer of snow outside.

I inhaled, steadying myself.

My dad was already moving toward the door, opening it before Liam even had the chance to knock.

“Morning, son,” my dad greeted, his voice even, assessing but not unkind.

“Morning, sir,” Liam replied smoothly, his posture adjusting just slightly—a reflex, a quiet acknowledgment of hierarchy.

I stepped forward, reaching for one of my bags, but before I could, Liam was already grabbing it for me.

“I got it,” he murmured.

Of course, he did.

My mother appeared in the hallway, wrapping herself in a thick sweater, her expression unreadable but expectant.

“Well,” she said, studying me for a moment. “That’s that, then.” “I guess so,” I said.
She reached for my coat sleeve, adjusting an invisible thread, smoothing a wrinkle that wasn’t there.

“Behave yourself,” she murmured, a warning laced with well-meaning concern.

I forced a smile. “Always.”

She gave me a long look, then sighed, stepping back. Approval was never really given, but it wasn’t withheld, either.

I turned to my dad next, expecting a simple “drive safe” and nothing more. Instead, he placed a firm, warm hand on my shoulder.
“You look good, kid,” he said, low enough that my mother wouldn’t interrupt. “More grown up. Keep up the good work.”

The words settled in my chest, heavier than I expected. I swallowed, nodding. “Thanks, Dad.”
He nodded back, eyes lingering for a second longer than normal. Then he turned to Liam, his expression unreadable.

“Take care of her.”

Liam’s grip on my bag tightened just slightly. “Always,” he said.
And that was it.

No long goodbyes, no dramatics—just a quiet exchange of words, a shift in the air, a passing of something unspoken.

I stepped outside, the cold air biting at my cheeks.

Liam opened the car door for me without a word, waiting until I was settled before shutting it behind me.


“Did my dad just call you ‘son,’ or am I hallucinating?”

Liam’s smirk was immediate, slow and entirely too self-satisfied. “He did.” I stared at him, waiting for further explanation that didn’t come.
“So that’s it?” I pressed. “You’re just part of the family now?” “Seems that way.”
I exhaled, shaking my head. “First the chess games, now this. What’s next, are you guys going to start tailgating together?”

Liam hummed, shifting the car into reverse. “I take it he told you?”

“Oh, you mean how you’ve been secretly playing chess with him for weeks and didn’t think to mention it? Yeah. He might’ve brought it up.”

Liam cast me a sidelong glance, completely unbothered. “And how did he say I was doing?” “Unbelievable,” I muttered, shaking my head.
“What?” he asked, glancing at me briefly before merging onto the road. “It wasn’t exactly relevant.”

“It wasn’t relevant?” I repeated, incredulous.

“Would you have preferred I announced it? ‘Hey, sweetheart, I’m currently hustling your father in chess—think I should pull back and let him win this one?’”

I dragged a hand down my face. “Oh my God.”

“Relax,” he said, entirely too pleased with himself. “He likes me.” “Yeah, apparently.”
I slumped back into my seat, still trying to process the fact that my father not only approved of Liam, but actively played chess with him.

I turned my head, glancing toward the backseat—and froze.

A bouquet of flowers rested on the seat. Elegant, fresh, carefully chosen. I frowned. Were they for me?
Or were they for me to give to his mother? The thought settled uneasily in my stomach.
Liam caught my gaze flickering toward them but said nothing, his smirk still playing at the edges of his mouth. Like he knew exactly what I was thinking but was going to make me wait for the answer.

Outside, the snow-covered roads stretched before us, the world shifting—pulling me away from what I had known and toward something else entirely.

Something bigger. Something undeniably his.
The roads stretched long and empty before us, the snowbanks piled high along the sides, untouched except for the occasional tire tracks cutting through. The car was warm, the quiet hum of the heater filling the space between us, but it wasn’t uncomfortable.

“So,” I started, finally relaxing against the seat. “I take it your Christmas was slightly less chaotic than mine?”

Liam huffed a small laugh, his fingers tapping idly against the steering wheel. “Depends. Define chaos.”

“Ben getting bullied into a round of poker after refusing to play Mario Kart. Maddie throwing herself onto the floor at least three times demanding I tell her every detail of our relationship
- she swears that it’s a thing of fairy tales. Ava queuing up a playlist that should be illegal in most countries, and me swallowing a Xanax with Jack trying to stay out of my mother’s line of fire.”

Liam nodded, “There it is.”

I exhaled slowly, watching the landscape blur past us. “Go ahead,” I said, not bothering to mask the exhaustion in my voice. “Say I told you so.”

He didn’t look over. “I don’t need to. You already know.”

He didn’t look at me. “I think it’s a miracle you didn’t end up in the ER.” My smile faltered. “It wasn’t like that.”
“You mixed benzos and alcohol, Caroline. Again.”

His voice didn’t rise, but it had that edge—the one that made people sit up straighter, the one that made professors second-guess their grading curves.

I stared at the dashboard. “I just needed the noise to stop for five minutes.” He inhaled slowly. “And if it hadn’t? What then?”
“Then I guess I’d still be down there pretending to care about charcuterie and family gossip.”

Liam shook his head, one hand loosening on the wheel before tightening again. “You don’t get to brush it off like that.”

“I’m not brushing it off,” I snapped, sharper than I meant. “I’m just trying not to drown in it.” That finally made him glance over.
“You don’t have to drown,” he said quietly. “You just have to listen. That’s all I’ve ever asked.”

I gave a brittle laugh. “No. You ask for obedience. Control. You don’t just want me to listen, Liam—you want me to hand over the wheel.”

