Chapter 43.




The eat-in kitchen was nothing like the formal dining room I had passed earlier. It was intimate, warm—not small by any means, but designed for family meals rather than grand entertaining.

A charcuterie board sat at the center of the wooden table, surrounded by flickering candles, the scent of freshly baked bread and seared beef mingling with the subtle spice of the wine Diane had selected. The atmosphere was effortlessly elegant, comfortable but curated.

Liam led me to the banquette, sitting beside me with a casual ease that made it seem like this was something we did every night. His hand rested on my knee, a silent reassurance, an anchor.

Diane smiled as she took her seat, her presence warm, commanding without being overbearing. “It’s rare that we all manage to be at the table at the same time,” she said, glancing between her sons. “Schedules have been busy.”

“All thanks to you, Mother, for making it happen,” Michael said, his voice teasing but appreciative as he poured himself a glass of wine.

“Flattery will get you everywhere,” she replied smoothly, lifting her own glass.

Senator Barnette took his seat at the head of the table, his movements deliberate, practiced. Not stiff, but composed—the kind of presence that never fully relaxed.

“Caroline,” he said, turning his attention to me. “Liam tells me you’re top of your class.” I nodded, forcing myself to match his steady gaze. “Yes, sir. I work hard to maintain it.”
“Caroline is studying International Relations,” Diane told the Senator, already reaching for the salad as the plates were passed around.

“And business,” I responded. “If the UN doesn’t work out, I want to ensure that I have the skills to manage an NGO.”

Diane’s expression brightened with interest. “That’s a noble path. Have you given any thought to what causes you’d like to contribute to outside of your studies?”

I hesitated, feeling the weight of her words. Outside of my studies.

“Youth in crisis,” I admitted, choosing my words carefully. “But I have yet to focus on a specific area, such as trauma relief or education access. I know service and philanthropy are integral to this family, for me, I’d like to focus on there is the greatest need for extra support.”

Diane’s lips curved. “I’d be happy to have you join me in some of my foundation work. We’ll find the right fit for you.”

Liam squeezed my knee, his silent way of saying, You’re doing well.

“Caroline’s always been the overachiever,” Liam said, his voice dripping with something both fond and smug. “It’s almost exhausting watching her.”

“And yet, somehow, she still manages to keep you in line,” Michael mused, smirking over his wine glass.

Liam shot him a look, but I could see the faint amusement tugging at the corner of his mouth.

“I imagine keeping Liam in line is no small task,” Senator Barnette remarked, his tone light, but his gaze sharp. “Are you sure you’re up for it?”

I held his gaze, my fingers tightening slightly against the stem of my wine glass. “I’m sure,” I said, without hesitation.
A pause. Then, just the slightest nod from him. Approval. Liam’s hand flexed on my knee.
“Tell me,” the Senator continued, shifting the conversation seamlessly, “do you follow global affairs closely?”

“Yes,” I answered, already anticipating where this was going.

Senator Barnette’s voice was smooth, conversational—but his gaze was anything but casual. He was watching me carefully, waiting.
“Good,” he said, setting his wine glass down. “Then tell me—what are your thoughts on the current political shifts in the EU?”

I didn’t hesitate, not because I was cocky but because these were the conversations that brought me to Holbrook, the ones I yearned for in my classes. Liam would never admit it, but even he and I would go a few rounds discussing the merits of different political theories.

“It’s a complicated landscape,” I said, choosing my words carefully, “The rise of nationalist sentiment in certain member states is challenging the foundation of the union. Trade agreements are being reevaluated, and we’re seeing a pushback against supranational governance in favor of domestic control.”

He nodded slightly, an acknowledgment but not an approval. He was waiting for more.

“But,” I continued, “while this kind of fragmentation poses economic and political risks, it isn’t unprecedented. Historically, we’ve seen similar cycles—when empires become too large, too centralized, there’s always a reactionary shift back to sovereignty.”

A flicker of something unreadable in his expression.

“A cyclical theory,” he noted. “You’re comparing it to Rome?”

I allowed myself a small smile. “Or the Holy Roman Empire. Or the Ottoman dissolution. When power centralizes too much, local identities reassert themselves.”

Michael let out a quiet laugh, shaking his head. “And here I thought this was going to be a nice, light dinner.”

“You’re free to change the topic, dear,” Diane said sweetly.

Michael sighed, taking a sip of his wine. “Oh no, I’m learning a lot. Keep going.” Senator Barnette ignored the exchange, leaning slightly forward.
“So, if this is a natural cycle, what do you predict will happen?”

“Rebalancing,” I answered. “There will be populist movements pushing for sovereignty, but in the end, economic interdependence will force cooperation. No nation in the modern world is an island—figuratively speaking.”

“And yet, nationalism thrives,” he countered.

“It does,” I agreed. “Because it appeals to emotion rather than logic. It provides a clear in- group and out-group, someone to blame when economic circumstances shift. But long-term stability favors cooperation over isolation. The only question is how much instability we’ll see before that happens.”

There was a beat of silence. Then, the slightest nod of approval. “Interesting,” he said, lifting his glass.
Liam’s fingers tightened slightly on my knee.

“What about trade sanctions?” he asked next. “Necessary diplomatic tool or ineffective posturing?”

“Depends on the target,” I said without missing a beat. “Sanctions against major economies often backfire, but against smaller states with heavy reliance on international trade, they can be a powerful bargaining chip.”

“And morally?”

“There’s no morality in politics,” I said smoothly. “Only interests.”

That made his lips twitch, the faintest glimmer of something amused. “Good answer.” Diane smiled, clearly pleased. “Liam mentioned you were well-read.”
“I try to be,” I said politely.

“You said earlier that you want to work with NGOs or the UN,” the senator continued, his tone shifting slightly. “Tell me, what do you believe is the greatest challenge facing global governance today?”

I paused to collect my thoughts, “Fragmentation,” I said. “Too many actors with competing interests. The UN was built on the idea that international cooperation could prevent conflict, but realpolitik still dominates decision-making. Sovereign states won’t sacrifice their own power for the sake of collective security unless there’s an immediate benefit.”

“And what philosophy shapes your worldview?”

A test. I took a slow sip of my water, choosing my words carefully.

“I believe in pragmatism,” I said. “That ideals are only useful if they can be applied. I believe in stability over sentimentality, and that power is best wielded by those who understand how to use it responsibly.”

Silence followed by the slightest approving nod. I exhaled slowly, feeling the tension in my shoulders lessen—not completely, but enough.

“You’ll do well,” he said simply, lifting his glass before turning to Michael. “See? Some intellectual stimulation never hurt anyone.”

“It’s refreshing,” Michael admitted, smirking. “Most of Liam’s past… acquaintances didn’t know the difference between a tariff and a trade agreement.”

Liam shot him a pointed look, but there was amusement there, too.

“Caroline isn’t most people,” he said smoothly, his fingers drumming my knee beneath the table.

I cleared my throat, suddenly aware that I had been speaking too much, too long.

“I’m sorry,” I said, setting my water goblet down. A small flush of warmth rose to my cheeks. “I tend to get carried away when discussing political landscapes.”

Senator Barnette’s expression didn’t change, but there was something in his eyes—approval, maybe, or at least acknowledgment.

“Nothing wrong with getting carried away,” Michael said, smirking over the rim of his glass. “Though I think this is the first time someone at this table has gotten more words in than Dad.”

Diane let out a soft laugh. “It’s refreshing.”

I glanced toward Liam, expecting him to be smug, but instead, his gaze was steady, warm— pleased. His fingers flexed slightly on my knee.

“Don’t apologize,” Liam murmured, low enough for only me to hear.

“It’s a good thing, Caroline,” Diane added, her voice laced with warmth. “Passion and intelligence are never something to shy away from.”

I exhaled slowly, my posture easing slightly, my shoulders relaxing.

“Speaking of passion,” Michael cut in, grinning as he leaned back in his chair. “Do you know how passionate Liam was about getting out of piano lessons when we were kids?”

Liam groaned, tilting his head back. “Oh, here we go.”

“Oh, it’s happening,” Michael said, nodding toward me. “Since Caroline’s getting the full Barnette experience, she might as well know the finer details of your childhood brilliance.”

Diane smiled, dabbing at her lips with her napkin. “It really was impressive, in a way. He turned avoiding practice into a full-time sport.”

I turned to Liam, my curiosity piqued. “You played piano?”

“Barely,” he muttered. “More like I spent three years outmaneuvering our piano instructor.” “Outmaneuvering?” I repeated, arching a brow.
“I wasn’t interested,” Liam said, shrugging. “So I found ways to make sure I was otherwise occupied when lessons started.”

