“Then I suppose we’ve found another way you’ll be useful around here,” Michael mused, smirking at Liam.

“She’s already useful,” Liam said, his fingers tightening slightly on my knee. Michael raised his hands in surrender, his smirk deepening.
“I didn’t say she wasn’t.”

I tried to ignore the warmth curling low in my stomach. Liam’s hand remained on my leg, solid and steady.
And for the first time all night, I stopped worrying about whether I belonged here. Because, maybe, I already did.
The warmth of dinner lingered as the plates were cleared, replaced with delicate espresso cups, a dark chocolate torte drizzled with raspberry sauce, and a plate of freshly baked madeleines.

“Liam, be a dear and pour the espresso,” Diane said, gesturing toward the silver tray set at the center of the table.

Liam exhaled through his nose but stood, reaching for the small porcelain cups, filling each one with smooth, dark liquid.

“Did you ever work in food service?” I teased, watching the effortless way he handled the task.

“Please,” Michael scoffed, accepting his cup. “Do you think Liam has ever waited on anyone in his life?”

“I’ve waited for plenty of things,” Liam said, setting a cup in front of me before resuming his seat.

“Mostly for people to shut up,” Michael added, smirking. I let out a quiet laugh, shaking my head.
“Now, that’s not true,” Diane interjected smoothly. “Liam was very patient as a child. He always found ways to get what he wanted without demanding it.”

“That’s one way of putting it,” Michael muttered into his espresso.

“And you,” Diane continued, turning toward her eldest son, “were an absolute terror as a toddler.”

Michael groaned. “No, no, let’s not do this.”

“Oh, let’s,” Liam said, smirking. “By all means, Mother, continue.” “What did he do?” I asked, intrigued.
Diane smiled, setting her cup down. “Michael had a very strong sense of independence. He refused to be carried, refused to be buckled into anything. He believed, quite passionately, that he could do everything on his own.”

“That sounds respectable,” Michael said, nodding approvingly. “Until he ran away at three years old,” Diane finished.
I blinked. “You ran away?”

“He packed a suitcase and everything,” Liam added, his smirk widening. “Where did you go?” I asked, trying not to laugh.
“The front yard,” Senator Barnette supplied, taking a slow sip of his espresso. “I had a plan,” Michael said defensively.
“You had a lunchbox full of Goldfish crackers and a pair of mittens in the middle of July,” Liam corrected.

“Details.”

“You got ten feet down the driveway before you sat down and fell asleep,” Diane said fondly. “And then cried when we brought him back inside,” Liam added.
“Well, obviously,” Michael said. “It was a failed mission.” I shook my head, smiling into my espresso.

“And yet, somehow, you still ended up being the responsible one,” Diane mused, arching a brow at her eldest son.

Michael grinned. “It’s called growth, Mother.”

“Mmm,” she hummed. “Liam, have you ever attempted to run away?” “No need,” Liam said smoothly. “I always got exactly what I wanted.” His fingers curled around mine under the table, squeezing once.
And I knew exactly what he meant.

“Alright, Caroline,” Michael said, setting his espresso cup down with a grin that told me I was about to be put on the spot. “Since we’ve now thoroughly embarrassed my childhood self, what stories do you have from your past? Any particularly humiliating moments we should know about?”

I let out a small laugh, shaking my head. “Oh, I don’t think I have anything that can top running away with Goldfish crackers and seasonal confusion.”

“There’s always something,” Michael pressed. “Come on, give us one. What’s your most ridiculous childhood moment?”

Liam’s fingers tightened slightly around mine under the table, a silent cue that he was enjoying this.

I bit my lip, sifting through memories, landing on one that was humiliating at the time but harmless now.

“Alright,” I sighed, rolling my eyes playfully. “When I was five, I was convinced I could be a Broadway star. But I had… a very limited understanding of musical theater.”

“Oh, this is already good,” Michael muttered, grinning.

“One night, my parents had some colleagues over for dinner,” I continued. “And I decided it was the perfect opportunity to make my grand debut.”

Diane tilted her head, amused. “A performance?” “A very dramatic one,” I admitted.
“And what song did you grace them with?” Liam asked, his smirk deepening. I inhaled slowly.
“The theme song to Titanic.”

Michael nearly choked on his drink. “Céline Dion?” he wheezed. “Oh no,” I said flatly. “Not just Céline Dion. I did the whole scene.”

“No,” Diane said, clearly delighted.

“I pulled a dining chair into the middle of the living room, stood on top of it, stretched my arms out, and belted My Heart Will Go On at full volume.”

“Jesus Christ,” Liam muttered, rubbing a hand over his jaw. “Please tell me there’s video of this,” Michael said, still laughing.
“Unfortunately, yes,” I said, sighing. “I have a cousin who thinks it’s the funniest thing in the world and plays it at every family gathering.”

“That cousin deserves a medal,” Michael declared.

“Wait! Why wasn’t I shown this video at Thanksgiving?,” Liam said, in mock offense.

“Because,” I said proudly. “I have blackmail against Ben and I know how to use it.” I smiled sweetly up at Liam, laughter trying to escape my tight lips.

“Smart girl,” Diane said, laughing softly.

“That is spectacular,” Michael said, shaking his head. “You’re fitting in just fine, Caroline.” And just like that, the last shred of tension melted away.
Liam’s fingers curled tighter around mine beneath the table.

And for the first time that night, I stopped worrying about proving myself. Because I already had.

The evening wound down naturally, the conversation settling into lighter topics, easy laughter lingering in the air like the scent of espresso and dark chocolate.

Diane smiled warmly as she set down her napkin. “This was lovely. We’ll have to do it again while you’re here, Caroline.”

“I’d love that,” I said sincerely, still feeling the warmth of Michael’s teasing, the quiet approval of the Senator, the steady reassurance of Liam’s touch.

“Now that we’ve all stuffed ourselves,” Michael said, stretching slightly, “I say we move to the lounge, but I suspect Liam has other plans.”

Liam shot him a look.

“Don’t be jealous, Michael,” Liam said smoothly, pulling my chair out for me as I stood.

“Jealous?” Michael smirked. “Not at all. I look forward to watching Caroline slowly come to terms with what a nightmare you are to live with.”

Liam exhaled sharply, but I saw the twitch of amusement at the corner of his mouth. “If I survive,” I teased.
“Oh, sweetheart,” Liam murmured, leaning in just slightly, his breath warm against my ear. “You’re thriving.”

I rolled my eyes, but my stomach flipped anyway.

Diane stood, smoothing the front of her dress. “Go relax, dear,” she said with an easy smile. “You’ve had quite the evening.”

“Are you sure I can’t help you clean up at all?” I offer

Diane shook her head firmly, “Absolutely not, go, I’m sure you and Liam would like a reprieve together.”

I let out a quiet breath, feeling the weight of the night settle over me—but this time, it wasn’t exhaustion. It was satisfaction.

Because I had done it.

I had held my own.

And, if the warmth in Diane’s eyes and the approving nod from the Senator meant anything, I had passed.

“Liam,” the Senator cleared his throat, “Before you retreat for the evening, a word in my office.”

Liam nodded as if they were going to discuss the weather. I glanced over at Michael for an indication of if this were a normal occurrence. Michael turned on his heels and walked towards the living room, scotch in hand.
